\id MAT Autogenerated BSB by bsb2usfm

\h Matthew
\toc1 Matthew
\toc2 Matthew
\toc3 MAT
\mt1 Matthew
\c 1
\s1 The Genealogy of Jesus
\r (\ref Ruth 4:18–22|RUT 4:18-22\ref*; \ref Luke 3:23–38|LUK 3:23-38\ref*)
\p \v 1 \add This is the\add* record of \add the\add* genealogy of Jesus Christ, \add the\add* son of David, \add the\add* son of Abraham:
\b 
\q1 \v 2 Abraham was the father of Isaac,
\q1 Isaac the father of Jacob,
\q1 and Jacob the father of Judah and his brothers.
\b 
\q1 \v 3 Judah was the father of Perez and Zerah by Tamar,
\q1 Perez the father of Hezron,
\q1 and Hezron the father of Ram.\f + \fr 1:3 \ft Greek \fqa Aram\ft , a variant of \fqa Ram\ft ; also in verse 4; see \ref 1 Chronicles 2:9–10|1CH 2:9-10\ref*.\f*
\b 
\q1 \v 4 Ram was the father of Amminadab,
\q1 Amminadab the father of Nahshon,
\q1 and Nahshon the father of Salmon.
\b 
\q1 \v 5 Salmon was the father of Boaz by Rahab,
\q1 Boaz the father of Obed by Ruth,
\q1 Obed the father of Jesse,
\q1 \v 6 and Jesse the father of David the king.
\p Next:
\b 
\q1 David was the father of Solomon by Uriah’s \add wife\add*,
\q1 \v 7 Solomon the father of Rehoboam,
\q1 Rehoboam the father of Abijah,
\q1 and Abijah the father of Asa.\f + \fr 1:7 \ft Greek \fqa Asaph\ft , a variant of \fqa Asa\ft ; also in verse 8; see \ref 1 Chronicles 3:10|1CH 3:10\ref*.\f*
\b 
\q1 \v 8 Asa was the father of Jehoshaphat,
\q1 Jehoshaphat the father of Joram,
\q1 and Joram the father of Uzziah.
\b 
\q1 \v 9 Uzziah was the father of Jotham,
\q1 Jotham the father of Ahaz,
\q1 and Ahaz the father of Hezekiah.
\b 
\q1 \v 10 Hezekiah was the father of Manasseh,
\q1 Manasseh the father of Amon,\f + \fr 1:10 \ft Greek \fqa Amōs\ft , a variant spelling of Amon; twice in this verse; see \ref 1 Chronicles 3:14|1CH 3:14\ref*.\f*
\q1 Amon the father of Josiah,
\q1 \v 11 and Josiah the father of Jeconiah and his brothers
\q2 at \add the time\add* of the exile to Babylon.
\p \v 12 After the exile to Babylon:
\b 
\q1 Jeconiah was the father of Shealtiel,
\q1 Shealtiel the father of Zerubbabel,
\q1 \v 13 Zerubbabel the father of Abiud,
\q1 Abiud the father of Eliakim,
\q1 and Eliakim the father of Azor.
\b 
\q1 \v 14 Azor was the father of Zadok,
\q1 Zadok the father of Achim,
\q1 and Achim the father of Eliud.
\b 
\q1 \v 15 Eliud was the father of Eleazar,
\q1 Eleazar the father of Matthan,
\q1 Matthan the father of Jacob,
\q1 \v 16 and Jacob the father of Joseph, the husband of Mary,
\q2 of whom was born Jesus, who is called Christ.
\p \v 17 In all, then, \add there were\add* fourteen generations from Abraham to David, fourteen from David to the exile to Babylon, and fourteen from the exile to the Christ.
\s1 The Birth of Jesus
\r (\ref Isaiah 7:10–16|ISA 7:10-16\ref*; \ref Luke 2:1–7|LUK 2:1-7\ref*)
\p \v 18 This is how the birth of Jesus Christ came about: His mother Mary was pledged in marriage to Joseph, but before they came together, she was found to be with child through \add the\add* Holy Spirit. \v 19 Because Joseph her husband was a righteous man and was unwilling to disgrace her publicly, he resolved to divorce her quietly.
\p \v 20 But after he had pondered these things, an angel of \add the\add* Lord appeared to him in a dream \add and\add* said, “Joseph, son of David, do not be afraid to embrace Mary \add as\add* your wife, for the \add One\add* conceived in her is from \add the\add* Holy Spirit. \v 21 She will give birth to a Son, and you are to give Him the name Jesus,\f + \fr 1:21 \fqa Jesus \ft means \fqa The LORD saves\ft .\f* because He will save His people from their sins.”
\p \v 22 All this took place to fulfill what the Lord had said through the prophet:
\b 
\q1 \v 23 “Behold, the virgin will be with child
\q2 and will give birth to a son,
\q1 and they will call Him Immanuel”\f + \fr 1:23 \ft Literally \fqa they will call His name Immanuel\ft ; \ref Isaiah 7:14|ISA 7:14\ref* (see also DSS)\f*
\q2 (which means, “God with us”\f + \fr 1:23 \ft See \ref Isaiah 7:14|ISA 7:14\ref*, Isaiah 8:8, and Isaiah 8:10.\f*).
\p \v 24 When Joseph woke up, he did as the angel of \add the\add* Lord had commanded him and embraced \add Mary as\add* his wife. \v 25 \add But\add* he had no union with her\f + \fr 1:25 \ft Literally \fqa he did not know her\f* until she gave birth to a Son. And he gave Him the name Jesus.
\c 2
\s1 The Pilgrimage of the Magi
\r (\ref Micah 5:1–6|MIC 5:1-6\ref*)
\p \v 1 After Jesus was born in Bethlehem in Judea, during \add the\add* time of King Herod, Magi from \add the\add* east arrived in Jerusalem, \v 2 asking, “Where is the \add One who\add* has been born King of the Jews? We saw His star in the east\f + \fr 2:2 \ft Or \fqa as it rose\f* and have come to worship Him.”
\p \v 3 When King Herod heard \add this\add*, he was disturbed, and all Jerusalem with him. \v 4 And when he had assembled all the chief priests and scribes of the people, he asked them where the Christ was to be born.
\p \v 5 “In Bethlehem in Judea,” they replied, “for this is what the prophet has written:
\b 
\q1 \v 6 ‘\add But\add* you, Bethlehem, \add in the\add* land of Judah,
\q2 are by no means least among the rulers of Judah,
\q1 for out of you will come a ruler
\q2 who will be the shepherd of My people Israel.’\f + \fr 2:6 \ref Micah 5:2|MIC 5:2\ref*; see also 2 Samuel 5:2.\f*”
\p \v 7 Then Herod called the Magi secretly \add and\add* learned from them the \add exact\add* time the star had appeared. \v 8 And sending them to Bethlehem, he said: “Go \add and\add* search carefully for the Child, and when you find \add Him\add*, report to me, so that I too may go \add and\add* worship Him.”
\p \v 9 After they had heard the king, they went on their way, and the star they had seen in the east went ahead of them until it stood over the place where the Child was. \v 10 When they saw the star, they rejoiced with great delight. \v 11 \add On\add* coming to the house, they saw the Child with His mother Mary, and they fell down \add and\add* worshiped Him. Then they opened their treasures \add and\add* presented Him with gifts \add of\add* gold and frankincense and myrrh.
\p \v 12 And having been warned in a dream not to return to Herod, they withdrew to their country by another route.
\s1 The Flight to Egypt
\r (\ref Hosea 11:1–7|HOS 11:1-7\ref*)
\p \v 13 When \add the Magi\add* had gone, an angel of \add the\add* Lord appeared to Joseph in a dream. “Get up!” he said. “Take the Child and His mother and flee to Egypt. Stay there until I tell you, for Herod is going to search for the Child to kill Him.”
\p \v 14 So he got up, took the Child and His mother by night, and withdrew to Egypt, \v 15 where he stayed until the death of Herod. This fulfilled what \add the\add* Lord had spoken through the prophet: “Out of Egypt I called My Son.”\f + \fr 2:15 \ref Hosea 11:1|HOS 11:1\ref*\f*
\s1 Weeping and Great Mourning
\r (\ref Jeremiah 31:1–25|JER 31:1-25\ref*)
\p \v 16 When Herod saw that he had been outwitted by the Magi, he was filled with rage. Sending orders, he put to death all the boys in Bethlehem and its vicinity \add who were\add* two years old and under, according to the time he had learned from the Magi. \v 17 Then what was spoken through the prophet Jeremiah was fulfilled:
\b 
\q1 \v 18 “A voice is heard in Ramah,
\q2 weeping and great mourning,
\q1 Rachel weeping \add for\add* her children
\q2 and refusing to be comforted,
\q2 because they are no more.”\f + \fr 2:18 \ref Jeremiah 31:15|JER 31:15\ref*\f*
\s1 The Return to Nazareth
\r (\ref Luke 2:39–40|LUK 2:39-40\ref*)
\p \v 19 After Herod died, an angel of \add the\add* Lord appeared in a dream to Joseph in Egypt. \v 20 “Get up!” he said. “Take the Child and His mother and go to \add the\add* land of Israel, for those seeking the Child’s life are now dead.”
\p \v 21 So \add Joseph\add* got up, took the Child and His mother, and went to \add the\add* land of Israel. \v 22 But when he learned that Archelaus was reigning in Judea in place of his father Herod, he was afraid to go there. And having been warned in a dream, he withdrew to the district of Galilee, \v 23 and he went \add and\add* lived in a town called Nazareth. So was fulfilled what was spoken through the prophets: “He will be called a Nazarene.”
\c 3
\s1 The Mission of John the Baptist
\r (\ref Isaiah 40:1–5|ISA 40:1-5\ref*; \ref Mark 1:1–8|MRK 1:1-8\ref*; \ref Luke 3:1–20|LUK 3:1-20\ref*; \ref John 1:19–28|JHN 1:19-28\ref*)
\p \v 1 In those days John the Baptist came, preaching in the wilderness of Judea \v 2 and saying, “Repent, for the kingdom of heaven is near.” \v 3 This is he who was spoken of through the prophet Isaiah:
\b 
\q1 “\add A\add* voice of one calling in the wilderness,
\q2 ‘Prepare the way for \add the\add* Lord,
\q2 make straight paths for Him.’”\f + \fr 3:3 \ref Isaiah 40:3|ISA 40:3\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p \v 4 John wore a garment of camel’s hair, with \add a\add* leather belt around his waist. His food was locusts and wild honey. \v 5 \add People\add* went out to him \add from\add* Jerusalem and all Judea and the whole region around the Jordan. \v 6 Confessing their sins, they were baptized by him in the Jordan River.
\p \v 7 But when \add John\add* saw many of the Pharisees and Sadducees coming to his \add place of\add* baptism, he said to them, “\add You\add* brood of vipers, who warned you to flee from the coming wrath? \v 8 Produce fruit, then, in keeping with repentance. \v 9 And \add do\add* not presume to say to yourselves, ‘We have Abraham \add as our\add* father.’ For I tell you that out of these stones God can raise up children for Abraham. \v 10 The axe lies ready at the root of the trees, and every tree that does not produce good fruit will be cut down and thrown into \add the\add* fire.
\p \v 11 I baptize you with water\f + \fr 3:11 \ft Or \fqa in water\f* for repentance, but after me will come \add One\add* more powerful than I, whose sandals I am not worthy to carry. He will baptize you with \add the\add* Holy Spirit and with fire.\f + \fr 3:11 \ft Or \fqa in the Holy Spirit and in fire\f* \v 12 His winnowing fork \add is\add* in His hand to clear His threshing floor and to gather His wheat into the barn; but He will burn up the chaff with unquenchable fire.”
\s1 The Baptism of Jesus
\r (\ref Mark 1:9–11|MRK 1:9-11\ref*; \ref Luke 3:21–22|LUK 3:21-22\ref*; \ref John 1:29–34|JHN 1:29-34\ref*)
\p \v 13 At that time Jesus came from Galilee to the Jordan to be baptized by \add John\add*. \v 14 But John tried to prevent Him, saying, “I need to be baptized by You, and \add do\add* You come to me?”
\p \v 15 \wj “Let \add it\add* be so now,”\wj* Jesus replied. \wj “It is fitting for us to fulfill all righteousness in this way.”\wj* Then \add John\add* permitted Him.
\p \v 16 As soon as \add Jesus\add* was baptized, He went up out of the water. Suddenly the heavens were opened,\f + \fr 3:16 \ft NA, BYZ, and TR \fqa the heavens were opened to Him\f* and He saw\f + \fr 3:16 \ft Or \fqa and he saw\ft ; see \ref John 1:32–33|JHN 1:32-33\ref*.\f* the Spirit of God descending like a dove and resting on Him. \v 17 And a voice from heaven said, “This is My beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased!”
\c 4
\s1 The Temptation of Jesus
\r (\ref Mark 1:12–13|MRK 1:12-13\ref*; \ref Luke 4:1–13|LUK 4:1-13\ref*)
\p \v 1 Then Jesus was led by the Spirit into the wilderness to be tempted by the devil. \v 2 After fasting forty days and forty nights, He was hungry.
\p \v 3 The tempter came to Him \add and\add* said, “If You are \add the\add* Son of God, tell these stones to become bread.”
\p \v 4 But \add Jesus\add* answered, \wj “It is written:\wj*
\q1 \wj ‘Man shall not live on bread alone,\wj*
\q2 \wj but on every word that comes from \add the\add* mouth of God.’\f + \fr 4:4 \ref Deuteronomy 8:3|DEU 8:3\ref*\f*”\wj*
\p \v 5 Then the devil took Him to the holy city and set Him on the pinnacle of the temple. \v 6 “If You are \add the\add* Son of God,” he said, “throw Yourself down. For it is written:
\b 
\q1 ‘He will command His angels concerning You,
\q2 and they will lift You up in \add their\add* hands,
\q1 so that You will not strike Your foot
\q2 against a stone.’\f + \fr 4:6 \ref Psalm 91:11–12|PSA 91:11-12\ref*\f*”
\p \v 7 Jesus replied, \wj “It is also written: ‘Do not put \add the\add* Lord your God \add to the test\add*.’\f + \fr 4:7 \ref Deuteronomy 6:16|DEU 6:16\ref*\f*”\wj*
\p \v 8 Again, the devil took Him to a very high mountain and showed Him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory. \v 9 “All this I will give You,” he said, “if You will fall down \add and\add* worship me.”
\p \v 10 \wj “Away from Me, Satan!”\wj* Jesus told him. \wj “For it is written: ‘Worship \add the\add* Lord your God and serve Him only.’\f + \fr 4:10 \ref Deuteronomy 6:13|DEU 6:13\ref*\f*”\wj*
\p \v 11 Then the devil left Him, and angels came and ministered to Him.
\s1 Jesus Begins His Ministry
\r (\ref Isaiah 9:1–7|ISA 9:1-7\ref*; \ref Mark 1:14–15|MRK 1:14-15\ref*; \ref Luke 4:14–15|LUK 4:14-15\ref*)
\p \v 12 When \add Jesus\add* heard that John had been imprisoned, He withdrew to Galilee. \v 13 Leaving Nazareth, He went \add and\add* lived in Capernaum, which \add is\add* by the sea in \add the\add* region of Zebulun and Naphtali, \v 14 to fulfill what was spoken through the prophet Isaiah:
\b 
\q1 \v 15 “Land of Zebulun
\q2 and land of Naphtali,
\q1 \add the\add* Way of \add the\add* Sea, beyond the Jordan,
\q2 Galilee of the Gentiles —
\q1 \v 16 the people living in darkness
\q2 have seen \add a\add* great light;
\q1 on those living in \add the\add* land \add of the\add* shadow of death,
\q2 a light has dawned.”\f + \fr 4:16 \ref Isaiah 9:1–2|ISA 9:1-2\ref*\f*
\p \v 17 From that time \add on\add* Jesus began to preach, \wj “Repent, for the kingdom of heaven is near.”\wj*
\s1 The First Disciples
\r (\ref Mark 1:16–20|MRK 1:16-20\ref*; \ref Luke 5:1–11|LUK 5:1-11\ref*; \ref John 1:35–42|JHN 1:35-42\ref*)
\p \v 18 As \add Jesus\add* was walking beside the Sea of Galilee, He saw two brothers, Simon called Peter and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the sea, for they were fishermen. \v 19 \wj “Come, follow Me,”\wj* \add Jesus\add* said, \wj “and I will make you fishers of men.”\wj* \v 20 And at once they left \add their\add* nets \add and\add* followed Him.
\p \v 21 Going on from there, He saw two other brothers, James \add son\add* of Zebedee and his brother John. \add They were\add* in a boat with their father Zebedee, mending their nets. \add Jesus\add* called them, \v 22 and immediately they left the boat and their father \add and\add* followed Him.
\s1 Jesus Heals the Multitudes
\r (\ref Mark 3:7–12|MRK 3:7-12\ref*; \ref Luke 6:17–19|LUK 6:17-19\ref*)
\p \v 23 \add Jesus\add* went throughout Galilee, teaching in their synagogues, preaching the gospel of the kingdom, and healing every disease and sickness among the people. \v 24 News about Him spread all over Syria, and \add people\add* brought to Him all who were ill with various diseases, those suffering acute pain, \add the\add* demon-possessed, \add those\add* having seizures, and \add the\add* paralyzed, and He healed them.
\p \v 25 Large crowds followed Him, \add having come\add* from Galilee, \add the\add* Decapolis,\f + \fr 4:25 \ft That is, the Ten Cities\f* Jerusalem, Judea, and beyond the Jordan.
\c 5
\s1 The Sermon on the Mount
\p \v 1 When Jesus saw the crowds, He went up on the mountain and sat down. His disciples came to Him, \v 2 and He began to teach them, saying:
\s1 The Beatitudes
\r (\ref Psalm 1:1–6|PSA 1:1-6\ref*; \ref Luke 6:20–23|LUK 6:20-23\ref*)
\q1 \v 3 \wj “Blessed \add are\add* the poor in spirit,\wj*
\q2 \wj for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.\wj*
\q1 \v 4 \wj Blessed \add are\add* those who mourn,\wj*
\q2 \wj for they will be comforted.\wj*
\q1 \v 5 \wj Blessed \add are\add* the meek,\wj*
\q2 \wj for they will inherit the earth.\f + \fr 5:5 \ft Or \fqa Blessed are those who exercise strength under control, for they will inherit the land\ft ; see \ref Psalm 37:11|PSA 37:11\ref*.\f*\wj*
\q1 \v 6 \wj Blessed \add are\add* those who hunger and thirst for righteousness,\wj*
\q2 \wj for they will be filled.\wj*
\q1 \v 7 \wj Blessed \add are\add* the merciful,\wj*
\q2 \wj for they will be shown mercy.\wj*
\q1 \v 8 \wj Blessed \add are\add* the pure in heart,\wj*
\q2 \wj for they will see God.\wj*
\q1 \v 9 \wj Blessed \add are\add* the peacemakers,\wj*
\q2 \wj for they will be called sons of God.\wj*
\q1 \v 10 \wj Blessed \add are\add* those who are persecuted because of righteousness,\wj*
\q2 \wj for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.\wj*
\p \v 11 \wj Blessed are you when \add people\add* insult you, persecute \add you\add*, and falsely say all kinds of evil against you because of Me.\wj* \v 12 \wj Rejoice and be glad, because great \add is\add* your reward in heaven, for in the same way they persecuted the prophets before you.\wj*
\s1 Salt and Light
\r (\ref Mark 9:49–50|MRK 9:49-50\ref*; \ref Luke 14:34–35|LUK 14:34-35\ref*; \ref Philippians 2:12–18|PHP 2:12-18\ref*)
\p \v 13 \wj You are the salt of the earth. But if the salt loses its savor, how can it be made salty again? It is no longer good for \add anything\add*, except to be thrown out \add and\add* trampled by men.\wj*
\p \v 14 \wj You are the light of the world. A city on a hill\f + \fr 5:14 \ft Literally \fqa A city lying on a hill\f* cannot be hidden.\wj* \v 15 \wj Neither do \add people\add* light a lamp and put it under a basket. Instead, \add they set it\add* on a stand, and it gives light to everyone in the house.\wj* \v 16 \wj In the same way, \add let\add* your light shine before men, that they may see your good deeds and glorify your Father in heaven.\wj*
\s1 The Fulfillment of the Law
\p \v 17 \wj \add Do\add* not think that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets. I have not come to abolish \add them\add*, but to fulfill \add them\add*.\wj* \v 18 \wj For I tell you truly, until heaven and earth pass away, not a single jot, not a stroke of a pen, will disappear from the Law until everything is accomplished.\wj*
\p \v 19 \wj So then, whoever breaks one of the least of these commandments and teaches others to do likewise will be called least in the kingdom of heaven; but whoever practices and teaches \add them\add* will be called great in the kingdom of heaven.\wj* \v 20 \wj For I tell you that unless your righteousness exceeds \add that\add* of the scribes and Pharisees, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven.\wj*
\s1 Anger and Reconciliation
\r (\ref Luke 12:57–59|LUK 12:57-59\ref*)
\p \v 21 \wj You have heard that it was said to the ancients, ‘Do not murder’\f + \fr 5:21 \ref Exodus 20:13|EXO 20:13\ref*; Deuteronomy 5:17\f* and ‘Anyone who murders will be subject to judgment.’\wj* \v 22 \wj But I tell you that anyone who is angry with his brother\f + \fr 5:22 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa without cause\ft .\f* will be subject to judgment. Again, anyone who says to his brother, ‘Raca,’\f + \fr 5:22 \fqa Raca \ft is an Aramaic expression of contempt.\f* will be subject to the Sanhedrin.\f + \fr 5:22 \ft Or \fqa the Council\f* But anyone who says, ‘You fool!’ will be subject to the fire of hell.\f + \fr 5:22 \ft Or \fqa the hell of fire\ft ; Greek \fqa the Gehenna of fire\f*\wj*
\p \v 23 \wj So if you are offering your gift at the altar and there remember that your brother has something against you,\wj* \v 24 \wj leave your gift there before the altar. First go \add and\add* be reconciled to your brother; then come \add and\add* offer your gift.\wj*
\p \v 25 \wj Reconcile quickly with your adversary, while you are still on the way \add to court\add*. Otherwise, \add he\add* may hand you over to the judge, and the judge \add may hand you over\add* to the officer, and you may be thrown into prison.\wj* \v 26 \wj Truly I tell you, you will not get out until you have paid the last penny.\f + \fr 5:26 \ft Greek \fqa kodrantēn\ft ; that is, a Roman copper coin worth about 1/64 of a denarius\f*\wj*
\s1 Adultery
\r (\ref Leviticus 18:1–30|LEV 18:1-30\ref*)
\p \v 27 \wj You have heard that it was said, ‘Do not commit adultery.’\f + \fr 5:27 \ref Exodus 20:14|EXO 20:14\ref*; Deuteronomy 5:18\f*\wj* \v 28 \wj But I tell you that anyone who looks at a woman to lust after her \add has\add* already committed adultery with her in his heart.\wj* \v 29 \wj If your right eye causes you to sin, gouge it out and throw \add it\add* away. It is better for you to lose one part of your body \add than for\add* your whole body to be thrown into hell.\f + \fr 5:29 \ft Greek \fqa Gehenna\ft ; also in verse 30\f*\wj* \v 30 \wj And if your right hand causes you to sin, cut it off and throw \add it\add* away. It is better for you to lose one part of your body \add than for\add* your whole body to depart into hell.\wj*
\s1 Divorce
\r (\ref Deuteronomy 24:1–5|DEU 24:1-5\ref*; \ref Luke 16:18|LUK 16:18\ref*)
\p \v 31 \wj It has also been said, ‘Whoever divorces his wife must give her a certificate of divorce.’\f + \fr 5:31 \ref Deuteronomy 24:1|DEU 24:1\ref*\f*\wj* \v 32 \wj But I tell you that anyone who divorces his wife, except for sexual immorality, brings adultery upon her.\f + \fr 5:32 \ft Or \fqa causes her to commit adultery\f* And he who marries a divorced woman commits adultery.\wj*
\s1 Oaths and Vows
\r (\ref Numbers 30:1–16|NUM 30:1-16\ref*)
\p \v 33 \wj Again, you have heard that it was said to the ancients, ‘Do not break your oath, but fulfill your vows to the Lord.’\f + \fr 5:33 \ref Numbers 30:2|NUM 30:2\ref*\f*\wj* \v 34 \wj But I tell you not to swear at all: \add either\add* by heaven, for it is God’s throne;\wj* \v 35 \wj \add or\add* by the earth, for it is His footstool; \add or\add* by Jerusalem, for it is \add the\add* city of the great King.\wj* \v 36 \wj Nor should you swear by your head, for you cannot make a single hair white or black.\wj* \v 37 \wj Simply let your ‘Yes’ \add be\add* ‘Yes,’ \add and your\add* ‘No,’ ‘No.’ Anything more comes from the evil \add one\add*.\f + \fr 5:37 \ft Or \fqa from evil\f*\wj*
\s1 Love Your Enemies
\r (\ref Leviticus 24:17–23|LEV 24:17-23\ref*; \ref Luke 6:27–36|LUK 6:27-36\ref*)
\p \v 38 \wj You have heard that it was said, ‘Eye for eye and tooth for tooth.’\f + \fr 5:38 \ref Exodus 21:24|EXO 21:24\ref*; Leviticus 24:20; Deuteronomy 19:21\f*\wj* \v 39 \wj But I tell you not to resist an evil \add person\add*. \add If\add* someone slaps you on your right cheek, turn to him the other also;\wj* \v 40 \wj if someone wants to sue you and take your tunic, let him have \add your\add* cloak as well;\wj* \v 41 \wj and if someone forces you to go one mile,\f + \fr 5:41 \ft Greek \fqa one milion\ft ; that is, a Roman mile, approximately 4,855 feet or 1,480 meters\f* go with him two \add miles\add*.\f + \fr 5:41 \ft Literally \fqa go with him two.\f*\wj* \v 42 \wj Give to the \add one\add* who asks you, and do not turn away from the \add one\add* who wants to borrow from you.\wj*
\p \v 43 \wj You have heard that it was said, ‘Love your neighbor’\f + \fr 5:43 \ref Leviticus 19:18|LEV 19:18\ref*\f* and ‘Hate your enemy.’\wj* \v 44 \wj But I tell you, love your enemies and pray for those who persecute you,\f + \fr 5:44 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa love your enemies, bless those who curse you, do good to those who hate you, and pray for those who spitefully accuse you and persecute you\ft ; see \ref Luke 6:27–28|LUK 6:27-28\ref*.\f*\wj* \v 45 \wj that you may be sons of your Father in heaven. He causes His sun \add to rise\add* on \add the\add* evil and \add the\add* good, and sends rain on \add the\add* righteous and \add the\add* unrighteous.\wj* \v 46 \wj If you love those who love you, what reward will you get? Do not even tax collectors do the same?\wj* \v 47 \wj And if you greet only your brothers, what are you doing more than others? Do not even Gentiles do the same?\wj*
\p \v 48 \wj Be perfect, therefore, as your heavenly Father is perfect.’’\wj*
\c 6
\s1 Giving to the Needy
\r (\ref Deuteronomy 15:7–11|DEU 15:7-11\ref*)
\p \v 1 \wj \add “\add*Be careful not to perform your righteous acts\f + \fr 6:1 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa charitable acts \ft or \fqa alms\f* before men to be seen by them. If \add you do\add*, you will have no reward from your Father in heaven.\wj*
\p \v 2 \wj So when you give to the needy, do not sound a trumpet before you, as the hypocrites do in the synagogues and on the streets, to be honored by men. Truly I tell you, they already have their full reward.\wj* \v 3 \wj But \add when\add* you give to the needy, \add do\add* not \add let\add* your left \add hand\add* know what your right hand is doing,\wj* \v 4 \wj so that your giving may be in secret. And your Father, who sees what is done in secret, will reward you.\wj*
\s1 The Lord’s Prayer
\r (\ref Luke 11:1–4|LUK 11:1-4\ref*)
\p \v 5 \wj And when you pray, do not be like the hypocrites. For they love to pray standing in the synagogues and on the street corners to be seen by men. Truly I tell you, they already have their full reward.\wj* \v 6 \wj But when you pray, go into your inner room, shut your door, \add and\add* pray to your Father, who \add is\add* unseen. And your Father, who sees what is done in secret, will reward you.\wj*
\p \v 7 \wj And \add when\add* you pray, do not babble on like pagans, for they think that by their many words they will be heard.\wj* \v 8 \wj \add Do\add* not be like them, for your Father knows what you need before you ask Him.\wj*
\p \v 9 \wj So then, this is how you should pray:\wj*
\q1 \wj ‘Our Father in heaven,\wj*
\q2 \wj hallowed be Your name.\wj*
\q1 \v 10 \wj Your kingdom come,\wj*
\q2 \wj Your will be done,\wj*
\q2 \wj on earth as \add it is\add* in heaven.\wj*
\q1 \v 11 \wj Give us this day our daily bread.\wj*
\q1 \v 12 \wj And forgive us our debts,\wj*
\q2 \wj as we also have forgiven our debtors.\wj*
\q1 \v 13 \wj And lead us not into temptation,\wj*
\q2 \wj but deliver us from the evil \add one\add*.\f + \fr 6:13 \ft Or \fqa from evil\ft ; BYZ and TR include \fqa For Yours is the kingdom and the power and the glory, forever. Amen.\f*’\wj*
\p \v 14 \wj For if you forgive men their trespasses, your heavenly Father will also forgive you.\wj* \v 15 \wj But if you do not forgive men their trespasses, neither \add will\add* your Father forgive yours.\wj*
\s1 Proper Fasting
\p \v 16 \wj When you fast, \add do\add* not be somber like the hypocrites, for they disfigure their faces to show men they are fasting. Truly I tell you, they already have their full reward.\wj* \v 17 \wj But \add when\add* you fast, anoint your head and wash your face,\wj* \v 18 \wj so that \add your\add* fasting will not be obvious to men, but only to your Father, who \add is\add* unseen. And your Father, who sees what is done in secret, will reward you.\wj*
\s1 Treasures in Heaven
\r (\ref Luke 12:32–34|LUK 12:32-34\ref*)
\p \v 19 \wj \add Do\add* not store up for yourselves treasures on earth, where moth and rust\f + \fr 6:19 \ft Or \fqa worm\ft ; also in verse 20\f* destroy, and where thieves break in and steal.\wj* \v 20 \wj But store up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where moth \add and\add* rust do not destroy, and where thieves do not break in \add and\add* steal.\wj* \v 21 \wj For where your treasure is, there your heart will be also.\wj*
\s1 The Lamp of the Body
\r (\ref Luke 11:33–36|LUK 11:33-36\ref*)
\p \v 22 \wj The eye is the lamp of the body. If your eyes are good,\f + \fr 6:22 \ft Literally \fqa If your eye is sound\ft ; see \ref Proverbs 22:9|PRO 22:9\ref*\f* your whole body will be full of light.\wj* \v 23 \wj But if your eyes are bad,\f + \fr 6:23 \ft Literally \fqa if your eye is evil\ft ; see \ref Proverbs 23:6|PRO 23:6\ref* and Proverbs 28:22\f* your whole body will be full of darkness. If then the light within you is darkness, how great \add is\add* that darkness!\wj*
\p \v 24 \wj No one can serve two masters: Either he will hate the one and love the other, or he will be devoted to \add the\add* one and despise the other. You cannot serve \add both\add* God and money.\wj*
\s1 Do Not Worry
\r (\ref Luke 12:22–31|LUK 12:22-31\ref*)
\p \v 25 \wj Therefore I tell you, \add do\add* not worry about your life, what you will eat or drink; or \add about\add* your body, what you will wear. Is not life more than food, and the body \add more\add* than clothes?\wj* \v 26 \wj Look at the birds of the air: They do not sow \add or\add* reap \add or\add* gather into barns, and yet your heavenly Father feeds them. \add Are\add* you not much more valuable than they?\wj* \v 27 \wj Who of you by worrying can add a single hour to his life?\f + \fr 6:27 \ft Or \fqa a single cubit to his height\ft ; a cubit was approximately 18 inches or 45 centimeters.\f*\wj*
\p \v 28 \wj And why do you worry about clothes? Consider how the lilies of the field grow: \add They do\add* not labor \add or\add* spin.\wj* \v 29 \wj Yet I tell you that not even Solomon in all his glory was adorned like one of these.\wj* \v 30 \wj If that is how God clothes the grass of the field, which is \add here\add* today and tomorrow \add is\add* thrown into the furnace, \add will He\add* not much more \add clothe\add* you, O \add you\add* of little faith?\wj*
\p \v 31 \wj Therefore \add do\add* not worry, saying, ‘What shall we eat?’ or ‘What shall we drink?’ or ‘What shall we wear?’\wj* \v 32 \wj For the Gentiles strive after all these things, \add and\add* your heavenly Father knows that you need them.\wj* \v 33 \wj But seek first the kingdom of God\f + \fr 6:33 \ft SBL, WH, and NE \fqa seek first His kingdom\ft ; see also \ref Luke 12:31|LUK 12:31\ref*.\f* and His righteousness, and all these things will be added unto you.\wj*
\p \v 34 \wj Therefore \add do\add* not worry about tomorrow, for tomorrow will worry about itself. Today \add has\add* enough trouble of its own.’’\wj*
\c 7
\s1 Judging Others
\r (\ref Luke 6:37–42|LUK 6:37-42\ref*; \ref Romans 14:1–12|ROM 14:1-12\ref*)
\p \v 1 \wj \add “Do\add* not judge, or you will be judged.\wj* \v 2 \wj For with the same judgment you pronounce, you will be judged, and with the measure you \add use\add*, it will be measured to you.\wj*
\p \v 3 \wj Why do you look at the speck in your brother’s eye but fail to notice \add the\add* beam in your \add own\add* eye?\wj* \v 4 \wj How can you say to your brother, ‘Let me take the speck out of your eye,’ while \add there is still\add* a beam in your \add own\add* eye?\wj* \v 5 \wj You hypocrite! First take the beam out of your \add own\add* eye, and then you will see clearly to remove the speck from your brother’s eye.\wj*
\p \v 6 \wj \add Do\add* not give dogs what is holy; \add do\add* not throw your pearls before swine. If you do, they may trample them under their feet, and then turn \add and\add* tear you to pieces.\wj*
\s1 Ask, Seek, Knock
\r (\ref Luke 11:5–13|LUK 11:5-13\ref*)
\p \v 7 \wj Ask, and it will be given to you; seek, and you will find; knock, and \add the door\add* will be opened to you.\wj* \v 8 \wj For everyone who asks receives; he who seeks finds; and to him who knocks, \add the door\add* will be opened.\wj*
\p \v 9 \wj Which of you, if his son asks for bread, will give him a stone?\wj* \v 10 \wj Or \add if\add* he asks for a fish, will give him a snake?\wj* \v 11 \wj So if you who are evil know how to give good gifts to your children, how much more \add will\add* your Father in heaven give good things to those who ask Him!\wj*
\p \v 12 \wj In everything, then, do to others as you would have them do to you. For this is \add the essence of\add* the Law and the Prophets.\wj*
\s1 The Narrow Gate
\r (\ref Luke 13:22–30|LUK 13:22-30\ref*)
\p \v 13 \wj Enter through the narrow gate. For wide \add is\add* the gate and broad \add is\add* the way that leads to destruction, and many enter through it.\wj* \v 14 \wj But small \add is\add* the gate and narrow the way that leads to life, and only a few find it.\wj*
\s1 A Tree and Its Fruit
\r (\ref Luke 6:43–45|LUK 6:43-45\ref*)
\p \v 15 \wj Beware of false prophets. \add They\add* come to you in sheep’s clothing, but inwardly they are ravenous wolves.\wj* \v 16 \wj By their fruit you will recognize them. Are grapes gathered from thornbushes, or figs from thistles?\wj* \v 17 \wj Likewise, every good tree bears good fruit, but a bad tree bears bad fruit.\wj* \v 18 \wj \add A\add* good tree cannot bear bad fruit, \add and a\add* bad tree cannot bear good fruit.\wj* \v 19 \wj Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down and thrown into \add the\add* fire.\wj* \v 20 \wj So then, by their fruit you will recognize them.\wj*
\p \v 21 \wj Not everyone who says to Me, ‘Lord, Lord,’ will enter the kingdom of heaven, but only he who does the will of My Father in heaven.\wj* \v 22 \wj Many will say to Me on that day, ‘Lord, Lord, did we not prophesy in Your name, and \add in\add* Your name drive out demons and perform many miracles?’\wj*
\p \v 23 \wj Then I will tell them plainly, ‘I never knew you; depart from Me, \add you\add* workers of lawlessness!’\wj*
\s1 The House on the Rock
\r (\ref Luke 6:46–49|LUK 6:46-49\ref*)
\p \v 24 \wj Therefore everyone who hears these words of Mine and acts on them is like \add a\add* wise man who built his house on the rock.\wj* \v 25 \wj The rain fell, the torrents raged, and the winds blew and beat against that house; yet it did not fall, because its foundation was on the rock.\wj*
\p \v 26 \wj But everyone who hears these words of Mine and does not act on them is like a foolish man who built his house on sand.\wj* \v 27 \wj The rain fell, the torrents raged, and the winds blew and beat against that house, and it fell— and great was its collapse!”\wj*
\s1 The Authority of Jesus
\p \v 28 When Jesus had finished saying these things, the crowds were astonished at His teaching, \v 29 because He taught as \add one\add* who had authority, and not as their scribes.
\c 8
\s1 The Leper’s Prayer
\r (\ref Leviticus 14:1–32|LEV 14:1-32\ref*; \ref Mark 1:40–45|MRK 1:40-45\ref*; \ref Luke 5:12–16|LUK 5:12-16\ref*)
\p \v 1 When \add Jesus\add* came down from the mountain, large crowds followed Him. \v 2 Suddenly a leper\f + \fr 8:2 \ft A \fqa leper \ft was one afflicted with a skin disease. See Leviticus 13.\f* came \add and\add* knelt before Him, saying, “Lord, if You are willing, You can make me clean.”
\p \v 3 \add Jesus\add* reached out \add His\add* hand \add and\add* touched \add the man\add*. \wj “I am willing,”\wj* He said. \wj “Be clean!”\wj* And immediately his leprosy was cleansed.
\p \v 4 Then Jesus instructed him, \wj “See that you don’t tell anyone. But go, show yourself to the priest and offer the gift prescribed by Moses, as a testimony to them.”\f + \fr 8:4 \ft See \ref Leviticus 14:1–32|LEV 14:1-32\ref*.\f*\wj*
\s1 The Faith of the Centurion
\r (\ref Luke 7:1–10|LUK 7:1-10\ref*; \ref John 4:43–54|JHN 4:43-54\ref*)
\p \v 5 When \add Jesus\add* had entered Capernaum, a centurion came \add and\add* pleaded with Him, \v 6 “Lord, my servant\f + \fr 8:6 \ft Or \fqa child\ft ; also in verses 8 and 13\f* lies at home, paralyzed \add and\add* in terrible agony.”
\p \v 7 \wj “I will go \add and\add* heal him,”\wj* \add Jesus\add* replied.
\p \v 8 The centurion answered, “Lord, I am not worthy to have You come under my roof. But just say the word, and my servant will be healed. \v 9 For I myself am a man under authority, with soldiers under me. I tell \add one\add* to go, and he goes, and another to come, and he comes. \add I tell\add* my servant to do something, and he does \add it\add*.”
\p \v 10 When Jesus heard \add this\add*, He marveled and said to those following \add Him\add*, \wj “Truly I tell you, I have not found anyone in Israel \add with\add* such great faith.\wj* \v 11 \wj I say to you that many will come from \add the\add* east and \add the\add* west to share the banquet with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob in the kingdom of heaven.\wj* \v 12 \wj But the sons of the kingdom will be thrown into the outer darkness, \add where\add* there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.”\wj*
\p \v 13 Then Jesus said to the centurion, \wj “Go! As you have believed, \add so\add* will it be done for you.”\wj* And his servant was healed at that very hour.
\s1 Jesus Heals at Peter’s House
\r (\ref Mark 1:29–34|MRK 1:29-34\ref*; \ref Luke 4:38–41|LUK 4:38-41\ref*)
\p \v 14 When Jesus arrived at Peter’s house, He saw \add Peter’s\add* mother-in-law sick in bed with a fever. \v 15 So He touched her hand, and the fever left her, and she got up and began to serve Him.
\p \v 16 When evening came, many who were demon-possessed were brought to \add Jesus\add*, and He drove out the spirits with a word and healed all the sick. \v 17 This was to fulfill what was spoken through the prophet Isaiah:
\b 
\q1 “He took up our infirmities
\q2 and carried our diseases.”\f + \fr 8:17 \ref Isaiah 53:4|ISA 53:4\ref*\f*
\s1 The Cost of Discipleship
\r (\ref Luke 9:57–62|LUK 9:57-62\ref*; \ref Luke 14:25–33|LUK 14:25-33\ref*; \ref John 6:59–66|JHN 6:59-66\ref*)
\p \v 18 \add When\add* Jesus saw \add a\add* large crowd around Him, He gave orders to cross to the other side \add of the sea\add*.\f + \fr 8:18 \ft That is, the Sea of Galilee; Greek \fqa to the other side\f* \v 19 And one of the scribes came to Him \add and\add* said, “Teacher, I will follow You wherever You go.”
\p \v 20 Jesus replied, \wj “Foxes have dens and birds of the air \add have\add* nests, but \add the\add* Son of Man has no \add place\add* to lay \add His\add* head.”\wj*
\p \v 21 Another of His disciples requested, “Lord, first let me go and bury my father.”
\p \v 22 But Jesus told him, \wj “Follow Me, and let the dead bury their own dead.”\wj*
\s1 Jesus Calms the Storm
\r (\ref Psalm 107:1–43|PSA 107:1-43\ref*; \ref Mark 4:35–41|MRK 4:35-41\ref*; \ref Luke 8:22–25|LUK 8:22-25\ref*)
\p \v 23 \add When\add* He got into the boat, His disciples followed Him. \v 24 Suddenly a violent storm came up on the sea, so that the boat was engulfed by the waves. But \add Jesus\add* was sleeping. \v 25 \add The disciples\add* went \add and\add* woke Him, saying, “Lord, save us! We are perishing!”
\p \v 26 \wj “\add You\add* of little faith,”\wj* \add Jesus\add* replied, \wj “why are you so afraid?”\wj* Then He got up \add and\add* rebuked the winds and the sea, and it was perfectly calm.
\p \v 27 \add The\add* men were amazed \add and\add* asked, “What kind \add of man\add* is this? Even the winds and the sea obey Him!”
\s1 The Demons and the Pigs
\r (\ref Mark 5:1–20|MRK 5:1-20\ref*; \ref Luke 8:26–39|LUK 8:26-39\ref*)
\p \v 28 When \add Jesus\add* arrived on the other side in the region of the Gadarenes,\f + \fr 8:28 \ft BYZ, TR, and PT \fqa Gergesenes\ft ; other manuscripts \fqa Gerasenes\f* He was met by two demon-possessed \add men\add* coming from the tombs. \add They were\add* so violent that no one could pass that way.
\p \v 29 “What do You \add want\add* with us, Son of God?” they shouted. “Have You come here to torture us before \add the\add* appointed time?”
\p \v 30 In the distance a large herd of pigs was feeding. \v 31 So the demons begged \add Jesus\add*, “If You drive us out, send us into the herd of pigs.”
\p \v 32 \wj “Go!”\wj* He told them. So they came out and went into the pigs, and the whole herd rushed down the steep bank into the sea and died in the waters.
\p \v 33 Those tending \add the pigs\add* ran off into the town \add and\add* reported all this, including the \add account\add* of the demon-possessed \add men\add*. \v 34 Then the whole town went out to meet Jesus. And when they saw Him, they begged \add Him\add* to leave their region.
\c 9
\s1 Jesus Heals a Paralytic
\r (\ref Mark 2:1–12|MRK 2:1-12\ref*; \ref Luke 5:17–26|LUK 5:17-26\ref*)
\p \v 1 \add Jesus\add* got into a boat, crossed over, and came to \add His\add* own town. \v 2 Just then \add some men\add* brought\f + \fr 9:2 \ft Literally \fqa And behold, they brought\f* to Him a paralytic lying on a mat. When Jesus saw their faith, He said to the paralytic, \wj “Take courage, son; your sins are forgiven.”\wj*
\p \v 3 On seeing this, some of the scribes said to themselves, “This \add man\add* is blaspheming!”
\p \v 4 But Jesus knew\f + \fr 9:4 \ft NA, BYZ, and TR \fqa saw\f* what they were thinking \add and\add* said, \wj “Why do you harbor evil in your hearts?\wj* \v 5 \wj Which is easier: to say, ‘Your sins are forgiven,’ or to say, ‘Get up and walk’?\wj* \v 6 \wj But so that you may know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins...”\wj* Then He said to the paralytic, \wj “Get up, pick up your mat, and go home.”\wj* \v 7 And \add the man\add* got up and went home.
\p \v 8 When the crowds saw this, they were filled with awe and glorified God, who had given such authority to men.
\s1 Jesus Calls Matthew
\r (\ref Mark 2:13–17|MRK 2:13-17\ref*; \ref Luke 5:27–32|LUK 5:27-32\ref*)
\p \v 9 As Jesus went on from there, He saw a man named Matthew sitting at the tax booth. \wj “Follow Me,”\wj* He told him, and \add Matthew\add* got up \add and\add* followed Him.
\p \v 10 Later, as \add Jesus\add* was dining at \add Matthew’s\add* house, many tax collectors and sinners came \add and\add* ate with \add Him\add* and His disciples. \v 11 When the Pharisees saw this, they asked His disciples, “Why \add does\add* your Teacher eat with tax collectors and sinners?”
\p \v 12 \add On\add* hearing this, \add Jesus\add* said, \wj “\add It is\add* not the healthy \add who\add* need a doctor, but the sick.\wj* \v 13 \wj But go \add and\add* learn what this means: ‘I desire mercy, not sacrifice.’\f + \fr 9:13 \ref Hosea 6:6|HOS 6:6\ref*\f* For I have not come to call \add the\add* righteous, but sinners.\f + \fr 9:13 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa but sinners, to repentance\ft ; see \ref Luke 5:32|LUK 5:32\ref*.\f*”\wj*
\s1 Questions about Fasting
\r (\ref Mark 2:18–20|MRK 2:18-20\ref*; \ref Luke 5:33–35|LUK 5:33-35\ref*)
\p \v 14 Then John’s disciples came to \add Jesus and\add* asked, “Why is it that we and the Pharisees fast so often,\f + \fr 9:14 \ft NE and WH do not include \fqa so often\ft .\f* but Your disciples \add do\add* not fast?”
\p \v 15 Jesus replied, \wj “How can the guests of the bridegroom mourn while \add He\add* is with them? But \add the\add* time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them; then they will fast.\wj*
\s1 The Patches and the Wineskins
\r (\ref Mark 2:21–22|MRK 2:21-22\ref*; \ref Luke 5:36–39|LUK 5:36-39\ref*)
\p \v 16 \wj No one sews a patch of unshrunk cloth on an old garment. For the patch will pull away from the garment, and a worse tear will result.\wj*
\p \v 17 \wj Neither do \add men\add* pour new wine into old wineskins. If they do, the skins will burst, the wine will spill, and the wineskins will be ruined. Instead, they pour new wine into new wineskins, and both are preserved.”\wj*
\s1 The Healing Touch of Jesus
\r (\ref Mark 5:21–43|MRK 5:21-43\ref*; \ref Luke 8:40–56|LUK 8:40-56\ref*)
\p \v 18 While \add Jesus\add* was saying these things, a synagogue leader came \add and\add* knelt before Him. “My daughter has just died,” he said. “But come \add and\add* place Your hand on her, and she will live.”
\p \v 19 So Jesus got up \add and\add* went with him, along with His disciples. \v 20 Suddenly a woman who had suffered from bleeding \add for\add* twelve years came up behind \add Him and\add* touched the fringe of His cloak. \v 21 She said to herself, “If only I touch His cloak, I will be healed.”
\p \v 22 Jesus turned and saw her. \wj “Take courage, daughter,”\wj* He said, \wj “your faith has healed you.”\wj* And the woman was healed from that very hour.
\p \v 23 When Jesus entered the house of the synagogue leader, He saw the flute players and the noisy crowd. \v 24 \wj “Go away,”\wj* He told them. \wj “The girl is not dead, but asleep.”\wj* And they laughed at Him.
\p \v 25 After the crowd had been put outside, \add Jesus\add* went in \add and\add* took \add the girl\add* by the hand, and \add she\add* got up. \v 26 And the news about this spread throughout that region.
\s1 Jesus Heals the Blind and Mute
\r (\ref Mark 7:31–37|MRK 7:31-37\ref*)
\p \v 27 As Jesus went on from there, two blind \add men\add* followed Him, crying out, “Have mercy on us, Son of David!”
\p \v 28 After \add Jesus\add* had entered the house, the blind \add men\add* came to Him. \wj “Do you believe that I am able to do this?”\wj* \add He\add* asked.
\p “Yes, Lord,” they answered.
\p \v 29 Then He touched their eyes \add and\add* said, \wj “According to your faith will it be done to you.”\wj* \v 30 And their eyes were opened. Jesus warned them sternly, \wj “See that no one finds out about this!”\wj* \v 31 But they went out and spread the news about Him throughout the land.
\p \v 32 As they were leaving, a demon-possessed man who was mute was brought to \add Jesus\add*. \v 33 And when the demon had been driven out, the \add man\add* began to speak. The crowds were amazed \add and\add* said, “Nothing like this has ever been seen in Israel!”
\p \v 34 But the Pharisees said, “\add It is\add* by the prince of demons \add that\add* He drives out demons.”
\s1 The Lord of the Harvest
\r (\ref Luke 10:1–12|LUK 10:1-12\ref*)
\p \v 35 Jesus went through all the towns and villages, teaching in their synagogues, preaching the gospel of the kingdom, and healing every disease and sickness. \v 36 When He saw the crowds, He was moved with compassion for them, because they were harassed and helpless, like sheep without a shepherd.
\p \v 37 Then He said to His disciples, \wj “The harvest \add is\add* plentiful, but the workers \add are\add* few.\wj* \v 38 \wj Ask the Lord of the harvest, therefore, to send out workers into His harvest.”\wj*
\c 10
\s1 The Twelve Apostles
\r (\ref Mark 3:13–19|MRK 3:13-19\ref*; \ref Luke 6:12–16|LUK 6:12-16\ref*)
\p \v 1 And calling His twelve disciples \add to Him\add*, \add Jesus\add* gave them authority over unclean spirits, so that they could drive them out and heal every disease and sickness.
\p \v 2 These are the names of the twelve apostles: first Simon, called Peter, and his brother Andrew; James \add son\add* of Zebedee, and his brother John; \v 3 Philip and Bartholomew; Thomas and Matthew the tax collector; James \add son\add* of Alphaeus, and Thaddaeus; \v 4 Simon the Zealot,\f + \fr 10:4 \ft Greek \fqa Simon the Cananean\f* and Judas Iscariot, \add who\add* betrayed \add Jesus\add*.\f + \fr 10:4 \ft Literally \fqa the one also having betrayed Him\f*
\s1 The Ministry of the Twelve
\r (\ref Mark 6:7–13|MRK 6:7-13\ref*; \ref Luke 9:1–6|LUK 9:1-6\ref*)
\p \v 5 These twelve Jesus sent out with \add the following\add* instructions: \wj “Do not go onto the road of the Gentiles \add or\add* enter any town of \add the\add* Samaritans.\wj* \v 6 \wj Go rather to the lost sheep of Israel.\wj* \v 7 \wj As you go, preach this message: ‘The kingdom of heaven is near.’\wj* \v 8 \wj Heal the sick, raise \add the\add* dead, cleanse \add the\add* lepers,\f + \fr 10:8 \ft A \fqa leper \ft was one afflicted with a skin disease. See Leviticus 13.\f* drive out demons. Freely you have received; freely give.\wj*
\p \v 9 \wj \add Do\add* not take along \add any\add* gold or silver or copper in your belts.\wj* \v 10 \wj \add Take\add* no bag for \add the\add* road, or second tunic, or sandals, or staff; for the worker \add is\add* worthy of his provisions.\wj*
\p \v 11 \wj Whatever town or village you enter, find out who is worthy there \add and\add* stay \add at his house\add*\f + \fr 10:11 \ft Literally \fqa stay there\f* until you move on.\wj* \v 12 \wj As you enter the home, greet its \add occupants\add*.\f + \fr 10:12 \ft Literally \fqa greet it\f*\wj* \v 13 \wj If the home is worthy, \add let\add* your peace rest on it, but if it is not, \add let\add* your peace return to you.\wj* \v 14 \wj And if anyone will not welcome you or heed your words, shake the dust off your feet when you leave that home or town.\wj* \v 15 \wj Truly I tell you, it will be more bearable for Sodom and Gomorrah on \add the\add* day of judgment than for that town.\wj*
\s1 Sheep among Wolves
\r (\ref 2 Timothy 1:3–12|2TI 1:3-12\ref*)
\p \v 16 \wj Behold, I am sending you out like sheep among wolves; therefore be as shrewd as snakes and as innocent as doves.\wj* \v 17 \wj But beware of men, for they will hand you over to \add their\add* councils and flog you in their synagogues.\wj* \v 18 \wj On My account you will be brought before governors and kings as witnesses to them and to the Gentiles.\wj* \v 19 \wj But when they hand you over, \add do\add* not worry about how \add to respond\add* or what to say. In that hour you will be given what to say.\wj* \v 20 \wj For it will not be you speaking, but the Spirit of your Father speaking through you.\wj*
\p \v 21 \wj Brother will betray brother to death, and a father \add his\add* child; children will rise against \add their\add* parents and have them put to death.\wj* \v 22 \wj You will be hated by everyone because of My name, but the \add one who\add* perseveres to \add the\add* end will be saved.\wj*
\p \v 23 \wj When they persecute you in one town, flee to the next. Truly I tell you, you will not reach \add all\add* the towns of Israel before the Son of Man comes.\wj*
\p \v 24 \wj A disciple is not above \add his\add* teacher, nor a servant above his master.\wj* \v 25 \wj \add It is\add* enough for a disciple to be like his teacher, and a servant like his master. If the head of the house has been called Beelzebul,\f + \fr 10:25 \ft WH \fqa Beezeboul\ft ; Vulgate \fqa Beelzebub\f* how much more the members of his household!\wj*
\s1 Fear God Alone
\r (\ref Luke 12:4–7|LUK 12:4-7\ref*)
\p \v 26 \wj So do not be afraid of them. For there is nothing concealed that will not be disclosed, and \add nothing\add* hidden that will not be made known.\wj* \v 27 \wj What I tell you in the dark, speak in the daylight; what is whispered in \add your\add* ear, proclaim from the housetops.\wj*
\p \v 28 \wj Do not be afraid of those who kill the body but cannot kill the soul. Instead, fear the \add One who\add* can destroy both soul and body in hell.\f + \fr 10:28 \ft Greek \fqa Gehenna\f*\wj*
\p \v 29 \wj \add Are\add* not two sparrows sold for a penny?\f + \fr 10:29 \ft Greek \fqa an assarion\ft ; that is, a Roman copper coin worth about 1/16 of a denarius\f* Yet not one of them will fall to the ground apart from the will of your Father.\wj* \v 30 \wj And even the very hairs of your head are all numbered.\wj* \v 31 \wj So do not be afraid; you are worth more than many sparrows.\wj*
\s1 Confessing Christ
\r (\ref Luke 12:8–12|LUK 12:8-12\ref*)
\p \v 32 \wj Therefore everyone who confesses Me before men, I will also confess him before My Father in heaven.\wj* \v 33 \wj But whoever denies Me before men, I will also deny him before My Father in heaven.\wj*
\s1 Not Peace but a Sword
\r (\ref Micah 7:1–6|MIC 7:1-6\ref*; \ref Luke 12:49–53|LUK 12:49-53\ref*)
\p \v 34 \wj \add Do\add* not assume that I have come to bring peace to the earth; I have not come to bring peace, but a sword.\wj* \v 35 \wj For I have come to turn\wj*
\q1 \wj ‘a man against his father,\wj*
\q2 \wj a daughter against her mother,\wj*
\q2 \wj a daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law.\wj*
\q1 \v 36 \wj A man’s enemies \add will be\add* the \add members\add*\wj*
\q2 \wj of his own household.’\f + \fr 10:36 \ref Micah 7:6|MIC 7:6\ref*\f*\wj*
\p \v 37 \wj Anyone who loves \add his\add* father or mother more than Me is not worthy of Me; anyone who loves \add his\add* son or daughter more than Me is not worthy of Me;\wj* \v 38 \wj and anyone who does not take up his cross and follow Me is not worthy of Me.\wj* \v 39 \wj Whoever finds his life will lose it, and whoever loses his life for My sake will find it.\wj*
\s1 The Reward of Service
\r (\ref 2 Kings 4:8–17|2KI 4:8-17\ref*)
\p \v 40 \wj He who receives you receives Me, and he who receives Me receives the \add One who\add* sent Me.\wj* \v 41 \wj Whoever receives a prophet because he is a prophet will receive a prophet’s reward, and whoever receives a righteous \add man\add* because he is a righteous \add man\add* will receive a righteous \add man’s\add* reward.\wj* \v 42 \wj And if anyone gives even a cup of cold \add water\add* to one of these little ones \add because he is My\add* disciple, truly I tell you, he will never lose his reward.”\wj*
\c 11
\s1 John’s Inquiry
\r (\ref Luke 7:18–23|LUK 7:18-23\ref*)
\p \v 1 After Jesus had finished instructing His twelve disciples, He went on from there to teach and preach in their cities.\f + \fr 11:1 \ft That is, in the towns of Galilee\f*
\p \v 2 Meanwhile John heard in prison \add about\add* the works of Christ, \add and\add* he sent his disciples\f + \fr 11:2 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa he sent two of his disciples\f* \v 3 to ask Him, “Are You the \add One who\add* was to come, or should we look for someone else?”
\p \v 4 Jesus replied, \wj “Go \add back and\add* report to John what you hear and see:\wj* \v 5 \wj \add The\add* blind receive sight, \add the\add* lame walk, \add the\add* lepers\f + \fr 11:5 \ft A \fqa leper \ft was one afflicted with a skin disease. See Leviticus 13.\f* are cleansed, \add the\add* deaf hear, \add the\add* dead are raised, and the good news is preached \add to the\add* poor.\wj* \v 6 \wj Blessed is the \add one who\add* does not fall away on account of Me.\f + \fr 11:6 \ft Or \fqa who is not offended by Me\f*”\wj*
\s1 Jesus Testifies about John
\r (\ref Malachi 3:1–5|MAL 3:1-5\ref*; \ref Luke 7:24–35|LUK 7:24-35\ref*)
\p \v 7 As \add John’s disciples\add* were leaving, Jesus began to speak to the crowds about John: \wj “What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed swaying in \add the\add* wind?\wj* \v 8 \wj Otherwise, what did you go out to see? A man dressed in fine \add clothes\add*? Look, those who wear fine \add clothing\add* are found in kings’ palaces.\wj* \v 9 \wj What then did you go out to see? A prophet? Yes, I tell you, and more than a prophet.\wj* \v 10 \wj This is \add the one\add* about whom it is written:\wj*
\q1 \wj ‘Behold, I will send My messenger ahead of You,\wj*
\q2 \wj who will prepare Your way before You.’\f + \fr 11:10 \ref Malachi 3:1|MAL 3:1\ref*\f*\wj*
\p \v 11 \wj Truly I tell you, among \add those\add* born of women there has risen no one greater than John the Baptist. Yet even the least in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he.\wj* \v 12 \wj From the days of John the Baptist until now, the kingdom of heaven has been subject to violence,\f + \fr 11:12 \ft Or \fqa has been forcefully advancing\f* and \add the\add* violent lay claim to it.\wj* \v 13 \wj For all the Prophets and the Law prophesied until John.\wj* \v 14 \wj And if you are willing to accept \add it\add*, he is \add the\add* Elijah who was to come.\f + \fr 11:14 \ft See \ref Malachi 4:5|MAL 4:5\ref*.\f*\wj*
\p \v 15 \wj He who has ears,\f + \fr 11:15 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa ears to hear\f* let him hear.\wj*
\p \v 16 \wj To what can I compare this generation? They are like children sitting in the marketplaces and calling out to others:\wj*
\q1 \v 17 \wj ‘We played the flute for you,\wj*
\q2 \wj and you did not dance;\wj*
\q1 \wj we sang a dirge,\wj*
\q2 \wj and you did not mourn.’\wj*
\p \v 18 \wj For John came neither eating nor drinking, and they say, ‘He has a demon!’\wj* \v 19 \wj The Son of Man came eating and drinking, and they say, ‘Look \add at this\add* glutton and drunkard, a friend of tax collectors and sinners!’ But wisdom is vindicated by her actions.”\wj*
\s1 Woe to the Unrepentant
\r (\ref Luke 10:13–16|LUK 10:13-16\ref*)
\p \v 20 Then \add Jesus\add* began to denounce the cities in which most of His miracles had been performed, because they did not repent. \v 21 \wj “Woe to you, Chorazin! Woe to you, Bethsaida! For if the miracles \add that\add* were performed in you had been performed in Tyre and Sidon, they would have repented long ago in sackcloth and ashes.\wj* \v 22 \wj But I tell you, it will be more bearable for Tyre and Sidon on \add the\add* day of judgment than for you.\wj*
\p \v 23 \wj And you, Capernaum, will you be lifted up to heaven? \add No\add*, you will be brought down to Hades! For if the miracles \add that\add* were performed in you had been performed in Sodom, it would have remained to this day.\wj* \v 24 \wj But I tell you that it will be more bearable for Sodom on \add the\add* day of judgment than for you.”\wj*
\s1 Rest for the Weary
\r (\ref Luke 10:21–24|LUK 10:21-24\ref*)
\p \v 25 At that time Jesus declared, \wj “I praise You, Father, Lord of heaven and earth, because You have hidden these things from \add the\add* wise and learned, and revealed them to little children.\wj* \v 26 \wj Yes, Father, for this was well-pleasing in Your sight.\wj*
\p \v 27 \wj All things have been entrusted to Me by My Father. No one knows the Son except the Father, \add and no one\add* knows the Father except the Son and those to whom the Son chooses to reveal \add Him\add*.\wj*
\p \v 28 \wj Come to Me, all you who are weary and burdened, and I will give you rest.\wj* \v 29 \wj Take My yoke upon you and learn from Me, for I am gentle and humble \add in\add* heart, and you will find rest \add for\add* your souls.\wj* \v 30 \wj For My yoke is easy and My burden is light.”\wj*
\c 12
\s1 The Lord of the Sabbath
\r (\ref 1 Samuel 21:1–7|1SA 21:1-7\ref*; \ref Mark 2:23–28|MRK 2:23-28\ref*; \ref Luke 6:1–5|LUK 6:1-5\ref*)
\p \v 1 At that time Jesus went through the grainfields on the Sabbath. His disciples were hungry and began to pick \add the\add* heads of grain and eat \add them\add*. \v 2 \add When the\add* Pharisees saw \add this\add*, they said to Him, “Look, Your disciples are doing what is unlawful on \add the\add* Sabbath.”
\p \v 3 \add Jesus\add* replied, \wj “Have you not read what David did when \add he\add* and his companions were hungry?\wj* \v 4 \wj He entered the house of God, and \add he and his companions\add* ate the consecrated bread,\f + \fr 12:4 \ft Or \fqa the Bread of the Presence\f* which was not lawful for \add them\add* to eat, but only for the priests.\wj*
\p \v 5 \wj Or haven’t you read in the Law that on the Sabbath the priests in the temple break the Sabbath and yet are innocent?\wj* \v 6 \wj But I tell you that One greater than the temple is here.\wj*
\p \v 7 \wj If only you had known the meaning of ‘I desire mercy, not sacrifice,’\f + \fr 12:7 \ref Hosea 6:6|HOS 6:6\ref*\f* you would not have condemned the innocent.\wj* \v 8 \wj For the Son of Man is Lord of the Sabbath.”\wj*
\s1 Jesus Heals on the Sabbath
\r (\ref Mark 3:1–6|MRK 3:1-6\ref*; \ref Luke 6:6–11|LUK 6:6-11\ref*)
\p \v 9 Moving on from there, \add Jesus\add* entered their synagogue, \v 10 and a man with a withered hand \add was there\add*. In order to accuse \add Jesus\add*, they asked Him, “Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbath?”
\p \v 11 He replied, \wj “If one of you has a sheep and it falls into a pit on the Sabbath, will he not take hold of it and lift \add it\add* out?\wj* \v 12 \wj How much more valuable is a man than a sheep! Therefore it is lawful to do good on the Sabbath.”\wj*
\p \v 13 Then \add Jesus\add* said to \add the\add* man, \wj “Stretch out your hand.”\wj* So he stretched \add it\add* out, and it was restored to full use, just like the other. \v 14 But the Pharisees went out \add and\add* plotted how they might kill \add Jesus\add*.
\s1 God’s Chosen Servant
\r (\ref Isaiah 42:1–9|ISA 42:1-9\ref*)
\p \v 15 Aware of this, Jesus withdrew from that place. Large crowds followed Him, and He healed them all, \v 16 warning them not to make Him known. \v 17 This was to fulfill what was spoken through the prophet Isaiah:
\b 
\q1 \v 18 “Here is My Servant,
\q2 whom I have chosen,
\q1 My beloved,
\q2 in whom My soul delights.
\q1 I will put My Spirit on Him,
\q2 and He will proclaim justice to the nations.
\q1 \v 19 He will not quarrel or cry out;
\q2 no one will hear His voice in the streets.
\q1 \v 20 A bruised reed He will not break,
\q2 and a smoldering wick He will not extinguish,
\q1 till He leads justice to victory.
\q2 \v 21 In His name \add the\add* nations will put their hope.”\f + \fr 12:21 \ref Isaiah 42:1–4|ISA 42:1-4\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\s1 A House Divided
\r (\ref Mark 3:20–27|MRK 3:20-27\ref*; \ref Luke 11:14–23|LUK 11:14-23\ref*)
\p \v 22 Then a demon-possessed man \add who was\add* blind and mute was brought to \add Jesus\add*, and He healed \add the man\add* so that \add he\add* could speak and see. \v 23 The crowds were astounded and asked, “Could this be the Son of David?”
\p \v 24 But when \add the\add* Pharisees heard this, they said, “Only by Beelzebul,\f + \fr 12:24 \ft WH \fqa Beezeboul\ft ; Vulgate \fqa Beelzebub\ft ; also in verse 27\f* \add the\add* prince of demons, \add does\add* this \add man\add* drive out demons.”
\p \v 25 Knowing their thoughts, \add Jesus\add* said to them, \wj “Every kingdom divided against itself will be laid waste, and every city or household divided against itself will not stand.\wj* \v 26 \wj If Satan drives out Satan, he is divided against himself. How then \add can\add* his kingdom stand?\wj* \v 27 \wj And if I drive out demons by Beelzebul, by whom \add do\add* your sons drive them out? So then, they will be your judges.\wj* \v 28 \wj But if I drive out demons by \add the\add* Spirit of God, then the kingdom of God has come upon you.\wj*
\p \v 29 \wj Or \add again\add*, how can anyone enter a strong \add man’s\add* house and steal his possessions, unless \add he\add* first ties up the strong \add man\add*? Then he can plunder his house.\wj*
\p \v 30 \wj He who is not with Me is against Me, and he who \add does\add* not gather with Me scatters.\wj*
\s1 The Unpardonable Sin
\r (\ref Mark 3:28–30|MRK 3:28-30\ref*)
\p \v 31 \wj Therefore I tell you, every sin and blasphemy will be forgiven men, but the blasphemy against \add the\add* Spirit \add will\add* not be forgiven.\wj* \v 32 \wj Whoever speaks a word against the Son of Man will be forgiven, but whoever speaks against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven, \add either\add* in this age \add or\add* in the \add one\add* to come.\wj*
\s1 Good and Bad Fruit
\r (\ref Luke 6:43–45|LUK 6:43-45\ref*)
\p \v 33 \wj Make a tree good and its fruit \add will be\add* good, or make a tree bad and its fruit \add will be\add* bad, for a tree is known by \add its\add* fruit.\wj* \v 34 \wj You brood of vipers, how can you who are evil say anything good? For out of the overflow of the heart the mouth speaks.\wj* \v 35 \wj The good man brings good things out of his good store of treasure, and the evil man brings evil things out of his evil store of treasure.\wj* \v 36 \wj But I tell you that men will give an account on \add the\add* day of judgment for every careless word they have spoken.\wj* \v 37 \wj For by your words you will be acquitted, and by your words you will be condemned.”\wj*
\s1 The Sign of Jonah
\r (\ref Jonah 3:1–10|JON 3:1-10\ref*; \ref Luke 11:29–32|LUK 11:29-32\ref*)
\p \v 38 Then some of the scribes and Pharisees said to Him, “Teacher, we want to see a sign from You.”
\p \v 39 \add Jesus\add* replied, \wj “A wicked and adulterous generation demands a sign, \add but\add* none will be given it except the sign of the prophet Jonah.\wj* \v 40 \wj For as Jonah was three days and three nights in the belly of the great fish, so the Son of Man will be three days and three nights in the heart of the earth.\wj*
\p \v 41 \wj The men of Nineveh will stand at the judgment with this generation and condemn it; for they repented at the preaching of Jonah, and now \add One\add* greater than Jonah is here.\wj* \v 42 \wj The Queen of \add the\add* South will rise at the judgment with this generation and condemn it; for she came from the ends of the earth to hear the wisdom of Solomon, and now \add One\add* greater than Solomon is here.\wj*
\s1 An Unclean Spirit Returns
\r (\ref Luke 11:24–26|LUK 11:24-26\ref*)
\p \v 43 \wj When an unclean spirit comes out of a man, it passes through arid places seeking rest and does not find \add it\add*.\wj* \v 44 \wj Then it says, ‘I will return to the house I left.’ On its return, it finds \add the house\add* vacant, swept clean, and put in order.\wj* \v 45 \wj Then it goes and brings with it seven other spirits more wicked than itself, and they go in \add and\add* dwell there. And the final \add plight\add* of that man is worse than the first. So will it be with this wicked generation.”\wj*
\s1 Jesus’ Mother and Brothers
\r (\ref Mark 3:31–35|MRK 3:31-35\ref*; \ref Luke 8:19–21|LUK 8:19-21\ref*)
\p \v 46 While \add Jesus\add* was still speaking to the crowds, His mother and brothers stood outside, wanting to speak to Him. \v 47 Someone told Him, “Look, Your mother and brothers are standing outside, wanting to speak to You.”\f + \fr 12:47 \ft WH does not include verse 47.\f*
\p \v 48 But \add Jesus\add* replied, \wj “Who is My mother, and who are My brothers?”\wj* \v 49 Pointing to His disciples, He said, \wj “Here are My mother and My brothers.\wj* \v 50 \wj For whoever does the will of My Father in heaven is My brother and sister and mother.”\wj*
\c 13
\s1 The Parable of the Sower
\r (\ref Mark 4:1–9|MRK 4:1-9\ref*; \ref Luke 8:4–8|LUK 8:4-8\ref*)
\p \v 1 That \add same\add* day Jesus went out of the house \add and\add* sat by the sea. \v 2 \add Such\add* large crowds gathered around Him that He got into a boat \add and\add* sat down, while all the people stood on the shore.
\p \v 3 And He told them many things in parables, saying, \wj “A farmer went out to sow his seed.\wj* \v 4 \wj And as he was sowing, some \add seed\add* fell along the path, and the birds came \add and\add* devoured it.\wj*
\p \v 5 \wj Some fell on rocky ground, where it did not have much soil. It sprang up quickly because the soil was shallow.\wj* \v 6 \wj But \add when the\add* sun rose, \add the seedlings\add* were scorched, and they withered because they had no root.\wj*
\p \v 7 \wj Other \add seed\add* fell among thorns, \add which\add* grew up and choked \add the seedlings\add*.\wj*
\p \v 8 \wj Still other \add seed\add* fell on good soil and produced a crop — a hundredfold, sixtyfold, \add or\add* thirtyfold.\wj*
\p \v 9 \wj He who has ears,\f + \fr 13:9 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa ears to hear\ft ; also in verse 43\f* let him hear.”\wj*
\s1 The Purpose of Jesus’ Parables
\r (\ref Isaiah 6:1–13|ISA 6:1-13\ref*; \ref Mark 4:10–12|MRK 4:10-12\ref*; \ref Luke 8:9–10|LUK 8:9-10\ref*)
\p \v 10 Then the disciples came to \add Jesus and\add* asked, “Why do You speak to \add the people\add* in parables?”
\p \v 11 He replied, \wj “The knowledge of the mysteries of the kingdom of heaven has been given to you, but not to them.\wj* \v 12 \wj Whoever has will be given \add more\add*, and he will have an abundance. Whoever \add does\add* not have, even what he has will be taken away from him.\wj* \v 13 \wj This is why I speak to them in parables:\wj*
\q1 \wj ‘Though seeing, they do not see;\wj*
\q2 \wj \add though\add* hearing, they do not hear \add or\add* understand.’\f + \fr 13:13 \ft See \ref Deuteronomy 29:4|DEU 29:4\ref*, Isaiah 42:20, Jeremiah 5:21, and Ezekiel 12:2.\f*\wj*
\p \v 14 \wj In them the prophecy of Isaiah is fulfilled:\wj*
\q1 \wj ‘You will be ever hearing but never understanding;\wj*
\q2 \wj you will be ever seeing but never perceiving.\wj*
\q1 \v 15 \wj For this people’s heart has grown callous;\wj*
\q2 \wj they hardly hear with \add their\add* ears,\wj*
\q2 \wj and they have closed their eyes.\wj*
\q1 \wj Otherwise they might see with \add their\add* eyes,\wj*
\q2 \wj hear with \add their\add* ears,\wj*
\q2 \wj understand with \add their\add* hearts,\wj*
\q1 \wj and turn, and I would heal them.’\f + \fr 13:15 \ref Isaiah 6:9–10|ISA 6:9-10\ref* (see also LXX)\f*\wj*
\p \v 16 \wj But blessed \add are\add* your eyes because they see, and your ears because they hear.\wj* \v 17 \wj For truly I tell you, many prophets and righteous \add men\add* longed to see what you see \add but\add* did not see \add it\add*, and to hear what you hear \add but\add* did not hear \add it\add*.\wj*
\s1 The Parable of the Sower Explained
\r (\ref Mark 4:13–20|MRK 4:13-20\ref*; \ref Luke 8:11–15|LUK 8:11-15\ref*)
\p \v 18 \wj Consider, then, the parable of the sower:\wj* \v 19 \wj When anyone hears the message of the kingdom but \add does\add* not understand \add it\add*, the evil one comes and snatches away what was sown in his heart. This is the \add seed\add* sown\f + \fr 13:19 \ft Literally \fqa the one sown\ft ; also in verses 20, 22, and 23\f* along the path.\wj*
\p \v 20 \wj The \add seed\add* sown on rocky ground is the \add one who\add* hears the word and at once receives it with joy.\wj* \v 21 \wj But since he has no root, he remains for only a season. \add When\add* trouble or persecution comes because of the word, he quickly falls away.\wj*
\p \v 22 \wj The \add seed\add* sown among the thorns is the \add one who\add* hears the word, but the worries of this life and the deceitfulness of wealth choke the word, and it becomes unfruitful.\wj*
\p \v 23 \wj But the \add seed\add* sown on good soil is the \add one who\add* hears the word \add and\add* understands \add it\add*. \add He\add* indeed bears fruit and produces a crop — a hundredfold, sixtyfold, or thirtyfold.”\wj*
\s1 The Parable of the Weeds
\r (\ref Ezekiel 17:1–10|EZK 17:1-10\ref*)
\p \v 24 \add Jesus\add* put before them another parable: \wj “The kingdom of heaven is like a man who sowed good seed in his field.\wj* \v 25 \wj But while everyone was asleep, his enemy came and sowed weeds among the wheat, and slipped away.\wj* \v 26 \wj When the wheat sprouted and bore grain, then the weeds also appeared.\wj*
\p \v 27 \wj The owner’s servants came to him \add and\add* said, ‘Sir, didn’t you sow good seed in your field? Where then did \add the\add* weeds \add come from\add*?’\wj*
\p \v 28 \wj ‘An enemy did this,’ he replied.\wj*
\p \wj \add So the\add* servants asked him, ‘Do you want us to go \add and\add* pull them up?’\wj*
\p \v 29 \wj ‘No,’ he said, ‘if you pull the weeds \add now\add*, you might uproot the wheat with them.\wj* \v 30 \wj Let both grow together until the harvest. At that time I will tell the harvesters: First collect the weeds and tie them in bundles to be burned; then gather \add the\add* wheat into my barn.’”\wj*
\s1 The Parable of the Mustard Seed
\r (\ref Mark 4:30–34|MRK 4:30-34\ref*; \ref Luke 13:18–19|LUK 13:18-19\ref*)
\p \v 31 He put before them another parable: \wj “The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed that a man took \add and\add* planted in his field.\wj* \v 32 \wj Although it is \add the\add* smallest of all seeds, yet it grows into the largest of garden plants and becomes a tree, so that the birds of the air come and nest in its branches.”\wj*
\s1 The Parable of the Leaven
\r (\ref Luke 13:20–21|LUK 13:20-21\ref*)
\p \v 33 He told them still another parable: \wj “The kingdom of heaven is like leaven that a woman took \add and\add* mixed into three measures of flour, until all of it was leavened.”\wj*
\s1 I Will Open My Mouth in Parables
\r (\ref Psalm 78:1–72|PSA 78:1-72\ref*)
\p \v 34 Jesus spoke all these things to the crowds in parables. He did not tell them anything without using a parable. \v 35 So was fulfilled what was spoken through the prophet:
\b 
\q1 “I will open My mouth in parables;
\q2 I will utter things hidden since \add the\add* foundation \add of the\add* world.”\f + \fr 13:35 \ref Psalm 78:2|PSA 78:2\ref* (see also LXX); SBL, NE, and WH do not include \fqa of the world\ft .\f*
\s1 The Parable of the Weeds Explained
\r (\ref Zephaniah 1:1–6|ZEP 1:1-6\ref*)
\p \v 36 Then \add Jesus\add* dismissed the crowds \add and\add* went into the house. His disciples came to Him \add and\add* said, “Explain to us the parable of the weeds in the field.”
\p \v 37 He replied, \wj “The \add One\add* who sows the good seed is the Son of Man.\wj* \v 38 \wj \add The\add* field is the world, and \add the\add* good seed represents the sons of the kingdom. \add The\add* weeds are the sons of the evil \add one\add*,\wj* \v 39 \wj and the enemy who sows them is the devil. The harvest is \add the\add* end of the age, and \add the\add* harvesters are angels.\wj*
\p \v 40 \wj As the weeds are collected and burned in the fire, so will it be at the end of the age.\wj* \v 41 \wj The Son of Man will send out His angels, and they will weed out of His kingdom every cause of sin and all who practice lawlessness.\wj* \v 42 \wj And they will throw them into the fiery furnace, \add where\add* there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.\wj* \v 43 \wj Then the righteous will shine like the sun in the kingdom of their Father.\f + \fr 13:43 \ft See \ref Daniel 12:3|DAN 12:3\ref*.\f*\wj*
\p \wj He who has ears, let him hear.\wj*
\s1 The Parables of the Treasure and the Pearl
\p \v 44 \wj The kingdom of heaven is like treasure hidden in a field. \add When a\add* man found \add it\add*, he hid \add it again\add*, and in \add his\add* joy he went and sold all he had and bought that field.\wj*
\p \v 45 \wj Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant in search of fine pearls.\wj* \v 46 \wj When he found one very precious pearl, he went away \add and\add* sold all he had and bought it.\wj*
\s1 The Parable of the Net
\p \v 47 \wj Once again, the kingdom of heaven is like a net that was cast into the sea and caught all kinds \add of fish\add*.\wj* \v 48 \wj When it was full, \add the men\add* pulled \add it\add* ashore. Then they sat down \add and\add* sorted the good \add fish\add* into containers, but threw the bad away.\wj*
\p \v 49 \wj So will it be at the end of the age: The angels will come and separate the wicked from the righteous\wj* \v 50 \wj and throw them into the fiery furnace, \add where\add* there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.\wj*
\p \v 51 \wj Have you understood all these things?”\wj*
\p “Yes,” they answered.
\p \v 52 Then He told them, \wj “For this reason, every scribe who has been discipled in the kingdom of heaven is like a homeowner who brings out of his storeroom new \add treasures\add* as well as old.”\wj*
\s1 The Rejection at Nazareth
\r (\ref Mark 6:1–6|MRK 6:1-6\ref*; \ref Luke 4:16–30|LUK 4:16-30\ref*)
\p \v 53 When Jesus had finished these parables, He withdrew from that place. \v 54 Coming to His hometown, He taught \add the people\add* in their synagogue, and they were astonished. “Where did this man get such wisdom and miraculous powers?” they asked. \v 55 “Isn’t this the carpenter’s son? Isn’t His mother’s name Mary, and \add aren’t\add* His brothers James, Joseph,\f + \fr 13:55 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa Joses\ft ; see \ref Mark 6:3|MRK 6:3\ref*.\f* Simon, and Judas? \v 56 Aren’t all His sisters with us as well? Where then \add did\add* this \add man get\add* all these things?” \v 57 And they took offense at Him.
\p But Jesus said to them, \wj “Only in \add his\add* hometown and in his own household is a prophet without honor.”\wj* \v 58 And He did not do many miracles there, because of their unbelief.
\c 14
\s1 The Beheading of John
\r (\ref Mark 6:14–29|MRK 6:14-29\ref*; \ref Luke 9:7–9|LUK 9:7-9\ref*)
\p \v 1 At that time Herod the tetrarch heard the reports about Jesus \v 2 and said to his servants, “This is John the Baptist; he has risen from the dead! That is why miraculous powers are at work in him.”
\p \v 3 \add Now\add* Herod had arrested John \add and\add* bound him and put \add him\add* in prison on account of Herodias, his brother Philip’s wife, \v 4 because John had been telling him, “It is not lawful for you to have her.” \v 5 Although \add Herod\add* wanted to kill \add John\add*, he was afraid of the people, because they regarded \add John\add* as a prophet.
\p \v 6 On Herod’s birthday, however, the daughter of Herodias danced before them and pleased Herod \v 7 so much that he promised with an oath to give to her whatever she asked.
\p \v 8 Prompted by her mother, she said, “Give me here on a platter the head of John the Baptist.”
\p \v 9 The king was grieved, but because of \add his\add* oaths and his guests, he ordered that \add her wish\add* be granted \v 10 and sent \add to have\add* John beheaded in the prison.
\p \v 11 \add John’s\add* head was brought in on a platter and presented to the girl, \add who\add* carried \add it\add* to her mother.
\p \v 12 Then \add John’s\add* disciples came \add and\add* took \add his\add* body and buried it. And they went \add and\add* informed Jesus.
\s1 The Feeding of the Five Thousand
\r (\ref Mark 6:30–44|MRK 6:30-44\ref*; \ref Luke 9:10–17|LUK 9:10-17\ref*; \ref John 6:1–15|JHN 6:1-15\ref*)
\p \v 13 When Jesus heard  \add about John \add*, He withdrew by boat privately to a solitary place. But the crowds found out \add about it and\add* followed Him on foot from the towns. \v 14 When He stepped ashore \add and\add* saw a large crowd, He had compassion on them and healed their sick.
\p \v 15 When evening came, the disciples came to Him \add and\add* said, “This is a desolate place, and the hour is already late. Dismiss the crowds so they can go to the villages \add and\add* buy themselves some food.”
\p \v 16 \wj “They do not need to go away,”\wj* Jesus replied. \wj “You give them \add something\add* to eat.”\wj*
\p \v 17 “We have here only five loaves of bread and two fish,” they answered.
\p \v 18 \wj “Bring them here to Me,”\wj* \add Jesus\add* said. \v 19 And He directed the crowds to sit down on the grass. Taking the five loaves and the two fish \add and\add* looking up to heaven, He spoke a blessing. Then He broke the loaves \add and\add* gave \add them\add* to the disciples, and the disciples \add gave them\add* to the people.
\p \v 20 They all ate and were satisfied, and \add the disciples\add* picked up twelve basketfuls of broken pieces that were left over. \v 21 About five thousand men were fed, besides women and children.
\s1 Jesus Walks on Water
\r (\ref Mark 6:45–52|MRK 6:45-52\ref*; \ref John 6:16–21|JHN 6:16-21\ref*)
\p \v 22 Immediately \add Jesus\add* made the disciples get into the boat and go on ahead of Him to the other side, while He dismissed the crowds. \v 23 After He had dismissed \add them\add*, He went up on the mountain by Himself to pray. When evening came, He was there alone, \v 24 but the boat was already far\f + \fr 14:24 \ft Greek \fqa many stadia\ft ; a stadion was about 607 feet or 185 meters\f* from land, buffeted by the waves because the wind was against it.
\p \v 25 During the fourth watch of the night,\f + \fr 14:25 \ft That is, between three and six in the morning\f* \add Jesus\add* went out to them, walking on the sea. \v 26 When the disciples saw Him walking on the sea, they were terrified. “It’s a ghost!” they said, and cried out in fear.
\p \v 27 But Jesus spoke up at once: \wj “Take courage! It is I. \add Do\add* not be afraid.”\wj*
\p \v 28 “Lord, if \add it\add* is You,” Peter replied, “command me to come to You on the water.”
\p \v 29 \wj “Come,”\wj* said \add Jesus\add*.
\p Then Peter got down out of the boat, walked on the water, and came toward Jesus. \v 30 But when he saw the strength of the wind,\f + \fr 14:30 \ft Literally \fqa when he saw the strong wind\ft ; NE and WH do not include \fqa strong\ft .\f* he was afraid and, beginning to sink, cried out, “Lord, save me!”
\p \v 31 Immediately Jesus reached out \add His\add* hand \add and\add* took hold of \add Peter\add*. \wj “\add You\add* of little faith,”\wj* He said, \wj “why did you doubt?”\wj*
\p \v 32 And when they had climbed back into the boat, the wind died down. \v 33 Then those who \add were\add* in the boat worshiped Him, saying, “Truly You are the Son of God!”
\s1 Jesus Heals at Gennesaret
\r (\ref Mark 6:53–56|MRK 6:53-56\ref*)
\p \v 34 When they had crossed over, they landed at Gennesaret. \v 35 And when the men of that place recognized \add Jesus\add*, they sent word to all the surrounding region. \add People\add* brought all the sick to Him \v 36 and begged Him just to let them touch the fringe of His cloak. And all who touched \add Him\add* were healed.
\c 15
\s1 The Tradition of the Elders
\r (\ref Mark 7:1–13|MRK 7:1-13\ref*)
\p \v 1 Then \add some\add* Pharisees and scribes came to Jesus from Jerusalem \add and\add* asked, \v 2 “Why \add do\add* Your disciples break the tradition of the elders? They do not wash their hands before they eat.”
\p \v 3 \add Jesus\add* replied, \wj “And why \add do\add* you break the command of God for the sake of your tradition?\wj* \v 4 \wj For God said, ‘Honor \add your\add* father and mother’\f + \fr 15:4 \ref Exodus 20:12|EXO 20:12\ref*; Deuteronomy 5:16\f* and ‘Anyone who curses \add his\add* father or mother must be put to death.’\f + \fr 15:4 \ref Exodus 21:17|EXO 21:17\ref*; Leviticus 20:9\f*\wj* \v 5 \wj But you say that if anyone says to \add his\add* father or mother, ‘Whatever you would have received from me is a gift devoted \add to God\add*,’\wj* \v 6 \wj he need not honor his father or mother \add with it\add*.\f + \fr 15:6 \ft NE and TR (see also \ref Mark 7:12|MRK 7:12\ref*); literally \fqa he need not honor his father or mother\ft ; SBL, NA, WH, and BYZ do not include \fqa or mother\ft .\f* Thus you nullify the word of God for the sake of your tradition.\wj* \v 7 \wj You hypocrites! Isaiah prophesied correctly about you:\wj*
\q1 \v 8 \wj ‘These people honor Me with their lips,\wj*
\q2 \wj but their hearts are far from Me.\wj*
\q1 \v 9 \wj They worship Me in vain;\wj*
\q2 \wj they teach \add as\add* doctrine \add the\add* precepts of men.’\f + \fr 15:9 \ref Isaiah 29:13|ISA 29:13\ref* (see also LXX)\f*”\wj*
\s1 What Defiles a Man
\r (\ref Mark 7:14–23|MRK 7:14-23\ref*)
\p \v 10 \add Jesus\add* called the crowd \add to Him and\add* said, \wj “Listen and understand.\wj* \v 11 \wj A man is not defiled by what enters \add his\add* mouth, but by what comes out of \add it\add*.”\wj*
\p \v 12 Then the disciples came to Him \add and\add* said, “Are You aware that the Pharisees were offended when they heard this?”
\p \v 13 But \add Jesus\add* replied, \wj “Every plant that My heavenly Father has not planted will be pulled up by its roots.\wj* \v 14 \wj Disregard them! They are blind guides.\f + \fr 15:14 \ft Or \fqa blind guides of the blind\f* If \add a\add* blind \add man\add* leads \add a\add* blind \add man\add*, both will fall into a pit.”\wj*
\p \v 15 Peter said to Him, “Explain this parable to us.”
\p \v 16 \wj “Do you still not understand?”\wj* \add Jesus\add* asked. \v 17 \wj “Do you not yet realize that whatever enters the mouth goes into the stomach and then is eliminated?\wj* \v 18 \wj But the things that come out of the mouth come from the heart, and these things defile a man.\wj* \v 19 \wj For out of the heart come evil thoughts, murder, adultery, sexual immorality, theft, false testimony, \add and\add* slander.\wj* \v 20 \wj These are what defile a man, but eating with unwashed hands does not defile \add him\add*.”\wj*
\s1 The Faith of the Canaanite Woman
\r (\ref Mark 7:24–30|MRK 7:24-30\ref*)
\p \v 21 Leaving that place, Jesus withdrew to the district of Tyre and Sidon. \v 22 And a Canaanite woman from that region came \add to Him\add*, crying out, “Lord, Son of David, have mercy on me! My daughter \add is\add* miserably possessed by a demon.”
\p \v 23 But \add Jesus\add* did not answer a word. So His disciples came \add and\add* urged Him, “Send her away, for she keeps crying out after us.”
\p \v 24 He answered, \wj “I was sent only to the lost sheep of \add the\add* house of Israel.”\wj*
\p \v 25 \add The woman\add* came \add and\add* knelt before Him. “Lord, help me!” she said.
\p \v 26 But \add Jesus\add* replied, \wj “It is not right to take the children’s bread and toss \add it\add* to the dogs.”\wj*
\p \v 27 “Yes, Lord,” she said, “even the dogs\f + \fr 15:27 \ft Or \fqa puppies\f* eat the crumbs that fall from their master’s table.”
\p \v 28 \wj “O woman,”\wj* Jesus answered, \wj “your faith is great! Let it be done for you as you desire.”\wj* And her daughter was healed from that very hour.
\s1 The Feeding of the Four Thousand
\r (\ref 2 Kings 4:42–44|2KI 4:42-44\ref*; \ref Mark 8:1–10|MRK 8:1-10\ref*)
\p \v 29 Moving on from there, Jesus went along the Sea of Galilee. Then He went up on a mountain \add and\add* sat down. \v 30 Large crowds came to Him, bringing \add the\add* lame, \add the\add* blind, \add the\add* crippled, \add the\add* mute, and many others, and laid them at His feet, and He healed them. \v 31 The crowd was amazed when they saw \add the\add* mute speaking, \add the\add* crippled restored, \add the\add* lame walking, and \add the\add* blind seeing. And they glorified the God of Israel.
\p \v 32 Then Jesus called His disciples \add to Him and\add* said, \wj “I have compassion for \add this\add* crowd, because they have already been with Me three days and have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry, or they may faint along the way.”\wj*
\p \v 33 The disciples replied, “Where in this desolate place could we \add find\add* enough bread to feed such a large crowd?”
\p \v 34 \wj “How many loaves do you have?”\wj* Jesus asked.
\p “Seven,” they replied, “and a few small fish.”
\p \v 35 And He instructed the crowd to sit down on the ground. \v 36 Taking the seven loaves and the fish, He gave thanks \add and\add* broke \add them\add*. Then He gave \add them\add* to \add the\add* disciples, and the disciples \add gave them\add* to the people.
\p \v 37 They all ate and were satisfied, and \add the disciples\add* picked up seven basketfuls of broken pieces that were left over. \v 38 A total of four thousand men were fed, besides women and children.
\p \v 39 After \add Jesus\add* had dismissed the crowds, He got into the boat and went to the region of Magadan.
\c 16
\s1 The Demand for a Sign
\r (\ref Mark 8:11–13|MRK 8:11-13\ref*; \ref Luke 12:54–56|LUK 12:54-56\ref*)
\p \v 1 Then the Pharisees and Sadducees came \add and\add* tested \add Jesus\add* by asking Him to show them a sign from heaven.
\p \v 2 But He replied, \wj “When evening comes, you say, ‘The weather \add will be\add* fair, for the sky is red,’\wj* \v 3 \wj and in the morning, ‘Today \add it will be\add* stormy, for the sky is red \add and\add* overcast.’ You know \add how\add* to interpret the appearance of the sky, but not the signs of the times.\f + \fr 16:3 \ft Several manuscripts do not include \fqa When evening comes... of the times. \ft from verses 2 and 3.\f*\wj* \v 4 \wj \add A\add* wicked and adulterous generation demands a sign, but none will be given it except the sign of Jonah.”\wj* Then He left them \add and\add* went away.
\s1 The Leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees
\r (\ref Mark 8:14–21|MRK 8:14-21\ref*; \ref Luke 12:1–3|LUK 12:1-3\ref*)
\p \v 5 When they crossed to the other side, the disciples forgot to take bread. \v 6 \wj “Watch out!”\wj* Jesus told them. \wj “Beware of the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees.”\wj*
\p \v 7 They discussed this among themselves \add and\add* concluded, “\add It is\add* because we did not bring \add any\add* bread.”
\p \v 8 Aware \add of their conversation\add*, Jesus said, \wj “\add You\add* of little faith, why are you debating among yourselves about having no bread?\wj* \v 9 \wj Do you still not understand? Do you not remember the five loaves for the five thousand, and how many basketfuls you gathered?\wj* \v 10 \wj \add Or\add* the seven loaves for the four thousand, and how many basketfuls you gathered?\wj* \v 11 \wj How do you not understand that I was not telling you about bread? But beware of the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees.”\wj*
\p \v 12 Then they understood that He was not telling \add them\add* to beware of the leaven used in bread, but of the teaching of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
\s1 Peter’s Confession of Christ
\r (\ref Mark 8:27–30|MRK 8:27-30\ref*; \ref Luke 9:18–20|LUK 9:18-20\ref*; \ref John 6:67–71|JHN 6:67-71\ref*)
\p \v 13 When Jesus came to the region of Caesarea Philippi, He questioned His disciples: \wj “Who \add do\add* people say the Son of Man is?”\wj*
\p \v 14 They replied, “Some \add say\add* John the Baptist; others \add say\add* Elijah; and still others, Jeremiah or one of the prophets.”
\p \v 15 \wj “But \add what about\add* you?”\wj* \add Jesus\add* asked. \wj “Who do you say I am?”\wj*
\p \v 16 Simon Peter answered, “You are the Christ, the Son of the living God.”
\p \v 17 Jesus replied, \wj “Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah!\f + \fr 16:17 \ft Greek \fqa Simon Bar-Jonah\f* For \add this\add* was not revealed to you \add by\add* flesh and blood, but \add by\add* My Father in heaven.\wj* \v 18 \wj And I tell you that you are Peter, and on this rock I will build My church, and \add the\add* gates of Hades will not prevail against it.\wj* \v 19 \wj I will give you the keys of the kingdom of heaven. Whatever you bind on earth will be bound in heaven, and whatever you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.”\wj*
\p \v 20 Then He admonished the disciples not to tell anyone that He was the Christ.
\s1 Christ’s Passion Foretold
\r (\ref Mark 8:31–33|MRK 8:31-33\ref*; \ref Luke 9:21–22|LUK 9:21-22\ref*)
\p \v 21 From that time \add on\add* Jesus\f + \fr 16:21 \ft NE and WH \fqa Jesus Christ\f* began to show His disciples that He must go to Jerusalem and suffer many things at the hands of the elders, chief priests, and scribes, and that He must be killed and on the third day be raised to life.
\p \v 22 Peter took Him aside \add and\add* began to rebuke Him. “Far be it from You, Lord!” he said. “This \add shall\add* never happen to You!”
\p \v 23 But \add Jesus\add* turned \add and\add* said to Peter, \wj “Get behind Me, Satan! You are a stumbling block to Me. For you do not have in mind the things of God, but the things of men.”\wj*
\s1 Take Up Your Cross
\r (\ref Mark 8:34–38|MRK 8:34-38\ref*; \ref Luke 9:23–27|LUK 9:23-27\ref*)
\p \v 24 Then Jesus told His disciples, \wj “If anyone wants to come after Me, he must deny himself and take up his cross and follow Me.\wj* \v 25 \wj For whoever wants to save his life will lose it, but whoever loses his life for My sake will find it.\wj* \v 26 \wj What will it profit a man if he gains the whole world, yet forfeits his soul? Or what \add can\add* a man give in exchange for his soul?\wj* \v 27 \wj For the Son of Man will come in His Father’s glory with His angels, and then He will repay each one according to what he has done.\wj*
\p \v 28 \wj Truly I tell you, some who are standing here will not taste death before they see the Son of Man coming in His kingdom.”\wj*
\c 17
\s1 The Transfiguration
\r (\ref Mark 9:1–13|MRK 9:1-13\ref*; \ref Luke 9:28–36|LUK 9:28-36\ref*; \ref 2 Peter 1:16–21|2PE 1:16-21\ref*)
\p \v 1 After six days Jesus took with \add Him\add* Peter, James, and John the brother of \add James\add*, and led them up a high mountain by themselves. \v 2 \add There\add* He was transfigured before them. His face shone like the sun, and His clothes became as white as the light.
\p \v 3 Suddenly Moses and Elijah appeared before them, talking with \add Jesus\add*. \v 4 Peter said to Jesus, “Lord, it is good for us to be here. If You wish, I will put up three shelters\f + \fr 17:4 \ft Or \fqa three tabernacles\f*— one for You, one for Moses, and one for Elijah.”
\p \v 5 While \add Peter\add* was \add still\add* speaking, a bright cloud enveloped them, and a voice from the cloud said, “This is My beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased.\f + \fr 17:5 \ft Cited in \ref 2 Peter 1:17|2PE 1:17\ref*\f* Listen to Him!” \v 6 When the disciples heard \add this\add*, they fell facedown in terror.
\p \v 7 Then Jesus came over and touched them. \wj “Get up,”\wj* He said. \wj “Do not be afraid.”\wj* \v 8 And when they looked up, they saw no one except Jesus.
\p \v 9 As they were coming down the mountain, Jesus commanded them, \wj “Do not tell anyone \add about this\add* vision until the Son of Man has been raised from \add the\add* dead.”\wj*
\p \v 10 The disciples asked Him, “Why then \add do\add* the scribes say that Elijah must come first?”
\p \v 11 \add Jesus\add* replied, \wj “Elijah \add does\add* indeed come, and he will restore all things.\wj* \v 12 \wj But I tell you that Elijah \add has\add* already come, and they did not recognize him, but have done to him whatever they wished. In the same way, the Son of Man will suffer at their hands.”\wj*
\p \v 13 Then the disciples understood that He was speaking to them about John the Baptist.
\s1 The Boy with a Demon
\r (\ref Mark 9:14–29|MRK 9:14-29\ref*; \ref Luke 9:37–42|LUK 9:37-42\ref*)
\p \v 14 When they came to the crowd, a man came up to \add Jesus and\add* knelt before Him. \v 15 “Lord, have mercy on my son,” he said. “He has seizures and is suffering terribly. He often falls into the fire \add or\add* into the water. \v 16 I brought him to Your disciples, but they could not heal him.”
\p \v 17 \wj “O unbelieving and perverse generation!”\wj* Jesus replied. \wj “How long must I remain with you? How long must I put up with you? Bring \add the boy\add* here to Me.”\wj* \v 18 Then Jesus rebuked \add the demon\add*, and \add it\add* came out of \add the boy\add*, and \add he\add* was healed from that moment.
\s1 The Power of Faith
\r (\ref Luke 17:5–10|LUK 17:5-10\ref*)
\p \v 19 Afterward the disciples came to Jesus privately \add and\add* asked, “Why couldn’t we drive it out?”
\p \v 20 \wj “Because you \add have\add* so little faith,”\wj* He answered. \wj “For truly I tell you, if you have faith the size of a mustard seed, you can say to this mountain, ‘Move from here to there,’ and it will move. Nothing will be impossible for you.”\wj*\f + \fr 17:20 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa \fv 21\fv*But this kind does not come out except by prayer and fasting\ft ; see \ref Mark 9:29|MRK 9:29\ref*.\f*
\s1 The Second Prediction of the Passion
\r (\ref Mark 9:30–32|MRK 9:30-32\ref*; \ref Luke 9:43–45|LUK 9:43-45\ref*)
\p \v 22 When they gathered together in Galilee, Jesus told them, \wj “The Son of Man is about to be delivered into \add the\add* hands of men.\wj* \v 23 \wj They will kill Him, and on the third day He will be raised \add to life\add*.”\wj* And \add the disciples\add* were deeply grieved.
\s1 The Temple Tax
\p \v 24 After they had arrived in Capernaum, the collectors of the two-drachma \add tax\add*\f + \fr 17:24 \ft Greek \fqa the didrachma\ft ; twice in this verse\f* came to Peter and asked, “\add Does\add* your Teacher pay the two drachmas?”
\p \v 25 “Yes,” he answered.
\p When \add Peter\add* entered the house, Jesus preempted him. \wj “What do you think, Simon?”\wj* He asked. \wj “From whom \add do\add* the kings of the earth collect customs \add and\add* taxes: from their \add own\add* sons, or from others?”\wj*
\p \v 26 “From others,” \add Peter\add* answered.
\p \wj “Then the sons are exempt,”\wj* Jesus said to him. \v 27 \wj “But so that we may not offend them, go to the sea, cast a hook, and take the first fish you catch. When you open its mouth, you will find a four-drachma coin.\f + \fr 17:27 \ft Greek \fqa a stater\ft ; that is, a silver coin worth approximately one shekel\f* Take \add it and\add* give \add it\add* to them for My tax and yours.”\wj*
\c 18
\s1 The Greatest in the Kingdom
\r (\ref Mark 9:33–41|MRK 9:33-41\ref*; \ref Luke 9:46–50|LUK 9:46-50\ref*)
\p \v 1 At that time the disciples came to Jesus \add and\add* asked, “Who then is \add the\add* greatest in the kingdom of heaven?”
\p \v 2 \add Jesus\add* invited a little child to stand among them. \v 3 \wj “Truly I tell you,”\wj* He said, \wj “unless you change and become like little children, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven.\wj* \v 4 \wj Therefore, whoever humbles himself like this little child is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven.\wj* \v 5 \wj And whoever welcomes a little child like this in My name welcomes Me.\wj*
\s1 Temptations and Trespasses
\r (\ref Mark 9:42–48|MRK 9:42-48\ref*; \ref Luke 17:1–4|LUK 17:1-4\ref*)
\p \v 6 \wj But if anyone causes one of these little ones who believe in Me \add to stumble\add*, it would be better for him to have a large millstone hung around his neck and to be drowned in the depths of the sea.\wj*
\p \v 7 \wj Woe to the world for the causes of sin. \add These\add* stumbling blocks must come, but woe to the man through whom \add they\add* come!\wj*
\p \v 8 \wj If your hand or your foot causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. It is better for you to enter life crippled or lame than to have two hands \add and\add* two feet \add and\add* be thrown into the eternal fire.\wj* \v 9 \wj And if your eye causes you to sin, gouge it out and throw it away. It is better for you to enter life with one eye than to have two eyes \add and\add* be thrown into the fire of hell.\f + \fr 18:9 \ft Or \fqa the hell of fire\ft ; Greek \fqa the Gehenna of fire\f*\wj*
\s1 The Parable of the Lost Sheep
\r (\ref Luke 15:1–7|LUK 15:1-7\ref*)
\p \v 10 \wj See \add that\add* you do not look down on any of these little ones. For I tell you that their angels in heaven always see the face of My Father in heaven.\f + \fr 18:10 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa \fv 11\fv*For the Son of Man came to save the lost\ft ; see \ref Luke 19:10|LUK 19:10\ref*.\f*\wj*
\p \v 12 \wj What \add do\add* you think? If a man has a hundred sheep and one of them goes astray, will he not leave the ninety-nine on the hills and go out to search for the \add one\add* that is lost?\wj* \v 13 \wj And if he finds it, truly I tell you, he rejoices more over \add that one sheep\add* than over the ninety-nine that did not go astray.\wj* \v 14 \wj In the same way, your Father in heaven is not willing that any of these little ones should perish.\wj*
\s1 A Brother Who Sins
\r (\ref Deuteronomy 19:15–21|DEU 19:15-21\ref*)
\p \v 15 \wj If your brother sins against you,\f + \fr 18:15 \ft NE and WH do not include \fqa against you\ft .\f* go \add and\add* confront him privately. If he listens to you, you have won your brother \add over\add*.\wj* \v 16 \wj But if he will not listen, take one or two others along, so that ‘every matter may be established by \add the\add* testimony of two or three witnesses.’\f + \fr 18:16 \ref Deuteronomy 19:15|DEU 19:15\ref*\f*\wj* \v 17 \wj If he refuses to listen to them, tell \add it\add* to the church. And if he refuses to listen even to the church, regard \add him\add* as you \add would\add* a pagan \add or\add* a tax collector.\wj*
\p \v 18 \wj Truly I tell you, whatever you bind on earth will be bound in heaven, and whatever you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.\wj*
\s1 Ask in My Name
\r (\ref John 16:23–33|JHN 16:23-33\ref*)
\p \v 19 \wj Again, I tell you truly that if two of you on the earth agree about anything \add you\add* ask for, it will be done for \add you\add* by My Father in heaven.\wj* \v 20 \wj For where two or three gather together in My name, there am I with them.”\wj*
\s1 The Unforgiving Servant
\r (\ref Romans 12:14–21|ROM 12:14-21\ref*)
\p \v 21 Then Peter came to \add Jesus and\add* asked, “Lord, how many times shall I forgive my brother who sins against me? Up to seven times?”
\p \v 22 Jesus answered, \wj “I tell you, not just seven times, but seventy-seven times!\wj*\f + \fr 18:22 \ft Or \fqa seventy times seven\ft ; see \ref Genesis 4:24|GEN 4:24\ref* LXX\f*
\p \v 23 \wj Because of this, the kingdom of heaven is like a king who wanted to settle accounts with his servants.\wj* \v 24 \wj As he began the settlements, a debtor owing ten thousand talents\f + \fr 18:24 \ft A talent was worth about twenty years’ wages for a laborer.\f* was brought to him.\wj* \v 25 \wj Since \add the man\add* was unable to pay, the master ordered that he be sold to pay \add his\add* debt, along with \add his\add* wife and children and everything he owned.\wj*
\p \v 26 \wj Then the servant fell on his knees before him. ‘Have patience with me,’ he begged, ‘and I will pay back everything.’\wj*
\p \v 27 \wj \add His\add* master had compassion \add on him\add*, forgave his debt, \add and\add* released him.\wj*
\p \v 28 \wj But when that servant went out, he found one of his fellow servants who owed him a hundred denarii.\f + \fr 18:28 \ft A denarius was customarily a day’s wage for a laborer; see \ref Matthew 20:2|MAT 20:2\ref*.\f* He grabbed him and began to choke \add him\add*, saying, ‘Pay back what you owe \add me\add*!’\wj*
\p \v 29 \wj So his fellow servant fell down \add and\add* begged him, ‘Have patience with me, and I will pay you \add back\add*.’\wj*
\p \v 30 \wj But he refused. Instead, he went \add and\add* had \add the man\add* thrown into prison until he could pay \add his\add* debt.\wj*
\p \v 31 \wj \add When\add* his fellow servants saw what had happened, they were greatly distressed, and they went \add and\add* recounted all \add of this\add* to their master.\wj*
\p \v 32 \wj Then the master summoned him \add and\add* said, ‘\add You\add* wicked servant! I forgave all \add your\add* debt because you begged me.\wj* \v 33 \wj Shouldn’t you have had mercy on your fellow servant, just as I \add had\add* on you?’\wj* \v 34 \wj In anger his master turned him over to the jailers to be tortured, until he should repay all that he owed.\wj*
\p \v 35 \wj That is how My heavenly Father will treat each of you unless you forgive \add your\add* brother from your heart.”\wj*
\c 19
\s1 Teachings about Divorce
\r (\ref Mark 10:1–12|MRK 10:1-12\ref*)
\p \v 1 When Jesus had finished saying these things, He left Galilee and went into the region of Judea beyond the Jordan. \v 2 Large crowds followed Him, and He healed them there.
\p \v 3 Then \add some\add* Pharisees came \add and\add* tested Him by asking, “Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for any reason?”
\p \v 4 \add Jesus\add* answered, \wj “Have you not read that from \add the\add* beginning the Creator ‘made them male and female,’\f + \fr 19:4 \ref Genesis 1:27|GEN 1:27\ref*; Genesis 5:2\f*\wj* \v 5 \wj and said, ‘For this reason a man will leave \add his\add* father and mother and be united to his wife, and the two will become one flesh’\f + \fr 19:5 \ref Genesis 2:24|GEN 2:24\ref* (see also LXX)\f*?\wj* \v 6 \wj So they are no longer two, but one flesh. Therefore what God has joined together, \add let\add* man not separate.”\wj*
\p \v 7 “Why then,” they asked, “\add did\add* Moses order \add a man\add* to give \add his wife\add* a certificate of divorce and send \add her\add* away?\f + \fr 19:7 \ft See \ref Deuteronomy 24:1|DEU 24:1\ref*.\f*”
\p \v 8 Jesus replied, \wj “Moses permitted you to divorce your wives because of your hardness of heart. But \add it\add* was not this way from \add the\add* beginning.\wj* \v 9 \wj Now I tell you that whoever divorces his wife, except for sexual immorality, and marries another woman commits adultery.\f + \fr 19:9 \ft SBL and BYZ include \fqa And he who marries a divorced woman commits adultery\ft ; see \ref Matthew 5:32|MAT 5:32\ref*.\f*”\wj*
\p \v 10 His disciples said to Him, “If this is the case between a man \add and his\add* wife, it is better not to marry.”
\p \v 11 \wj “Not everyone can accept this word,”\wj* He replied, \wj “but only \add those\add* to whom it has been given.\wj* \v 12 \wj For there are eunuchs who were born that way; \add others\add* were made \add that way\add* by men; and \add still others\add* live like eunuchs for the sake of the kingdom of heaven. The \add one\add* who can accept \add this\add* should accept \add it\add*.”\wj*
\s1 Jesus Blesses the Children
\r (\ref Mark 10:13–16|MRK 10:13-16\ref*; \ref Luke 18:15–17|LUK 18:15-17\ref*)
\p \v 13 Then little children were brought to \add Jesus\add* for Him to place \add His\add* hands on them and pray \add for them\add*. And the disciples rebuked those \add who brought them\add*. \v 14 But Jesus said, \wj “Let the little children come to Me, and do not hinder them! For the kingdom of heaven belongs to such as these.”\wj* \v 15 And after He had placed \add His\add* hands on them, He went on from there.
\s1 The Rich Young Man
\r (\ref Mark 10:17–31|MRK 10:17-31\ref*; \ref Luke 18:18–30|LUK 18:18-30\ref*)
\p \v 16 Just then a man came up to \add Jesus and\add* inquired, “Teacher, what good \add thing\add* must I do to obtain eternal life?”
\p \v 17 \wj “Why do you ask Me about what is good?”\f + \fr 19:17 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa \fv 16\fv*...“Good Teacher, what good thing must I do to obtain eternal life?” \fv 17\fv*“Why do you call Me good?”\ft See \ref Mark 10:17–18|MRK 10:17-18\ref* and Luke 18:18–19.\f*\wj* \add Jesus\add* replied. \wj “There is only One who is good. If you want to enter life, keep the commandments.”\wj*
\p \v 18 “Which \add ones\add*?” \add the man\add* asked.
\p Jesus answered, \wj “‘Do not murder, do not commit adultery, do not steal, do not bear false witness,\wj* \v 19 \wj honor \add your\add* father and mother, and love your neighbor as yourself.’\f + \fr 19:19 \ref Exodus 20:12–16|EXO 20:12-16\ref*; Leviticus 19:18; Deuteronomy 5:16–20\f*”\wj*
\p \v 20 “All these I have kept,” said the young man. “What do I still lack?”
\p \v 21 Jesus told him, \wj “If you want to be perfect, go, sell your possessions and give to the poor, and you will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow Me.”\wj*
\p \v 22 \add When\add* the young man heard this, he went away in sorrow, because he had great wealth.
\p \v 23 Then Jesus said to His disciples, \wj “Truly I tell you, it is hard for a rich man to enter the kingdom of heaven.\wj* \v 24 \wj Again I tell you, \add it\add* is easier for a camel to pass through \add the\add* eye of a needle than for a rich man to enter the kingdom of God.”\wj*
\p \v 25 When the disciples heard this, \add they were\add* greatly astonished \add and\add* asked, “Who then can be saved?”
\p \v 26 Jesus looked at \add them and\add* said, \wj “With man this is impossible, but with God all things \add are\add* possible.”\wj*
\p \v 27 “Look,” Peter replied, “we have left everything to follow You. What then will there be for us?”
\p \v 28 Jesus said to them, \wj “Truly I tell you, in the renewal of all things,\f + \fr 19:28 \ft Or \fqa in the regeneration\f* when the Son of Man sits on His glorious throne, you who have followed Me will also sit on twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel.\wj* \v 29 \wj And everyone who has left houses or brothers or sisters or father or mother or wife\f + \fr 19:29 \ft NE, WH, and NA do not include \fqa or wife\ft .\f* or children or fields for the sake of My name will receive a hundredfold and will inherit eternal life.\wj* \v 30 \wj But many \add who are\add* first will be last, and \add the\add* last \add will be\add* first.’’\wj*
\c 20
\s1 The Parable of the Workers
\p \v 1 \wj \add “\add*For the kingdom of heaven is like a landowner who went out early in \add the\add* morning to hire workers for his vineyard.\wj* \v 2 \wj He agreed \add to pay them\add* a denarius\f + \fr 20:2 \ft A denarius was customarily a day’s wage for a laborer; similarly in verses 9, 10, and 13.\f* for the day \add and\add* sent them into his vineyard.\wj*
\p \v 3 \wj About the third hour\f + \fr 20:3 \ft That is, about nine in the morning\f* he went out \add and\add* saw others standing in the marketplace doing nothing.\wj* \v 4 \wj ‘You also go into \add my\add* vineyard,’ he said, ‘and I will pay you whatever is right.’\wj* \v 5 \wj So they went.\wj*
\p \wj He went out again about \add the\add* sixth \add hour\add* and \add the\add* ninth hour\f + \fr 20:5 \ft That is, about noon and again about three in the afternoon\f* \add and\add* did the same thing.\wj*
\p \v 6 \wj About the eleventh \add hour\add*\f + \fr 20:6 \ft That is, about five in the afternoon; also in verse 9\f* he went out \add and\add* found still others standing \add around\add*. ‘Why have you been standing here all day \add long\add* doing nothing?’ he asked.\wj*
\p \v 7 \wj ‘Because no one has hired us,’ they answered.\wj*
\p \wj So he told them, ‘You also go into \add my\add* vineyard.’\f + \fr 20:7 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa and whatever is right, you shall receive.\f*\wj*
\p \v 8 \wj When evening came, the owner of the vineyard said to his foreman, ‘Call the workers and pay them \add their\add* wages, starting with the last \add ones hired\add* and moving on to the first.’\wj*
\p \v 9 \wj \add The workers who were hired\add* about the eleventh hour came \add and\add* each received a denarius.\wj* \v 10 \wj So when the original \add workers\add* came, they assumed they would receive more. But each of them also received a denarius.\wj*
\p \v 11 \wj On receiving \add their pay\add*, they began to grumble against the landowner.\wj* \v 12 \wj ‘These \add men who were hired\add* last worked only one hour,’ they said, ‘and you have made them equal to us who have borne the burden and the scorching heat of the day.’\wj*
\p \v 13 \wj But he answered one of them, ‘Friend, I am not being unfair to you. Did you not agree with me on one denarius?\wj* \v 14 \wj Take your \add pay\add* and go. I want to give this last \add man\add* the same as \add I gave\add* you.\wj* \v 15 \wj Do I not have the right to do as I please with what \add is\add* mine? Or are you envious because I am generous?’\wj*
\p \v 16 \wj So the last will be first, and the first \add will be\add* last.”\wj*\f + \fr 20:16 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa For many are called, but few are chosen.\f*
\s1 The Third Prediction of the Passion
\r (\ref Mark 10:32–34|MRK 10:32-34\ref*; \ref Luke 18:31–34|LUK 18:31-34\ref*)
\p \v 17 As Jesus was going up to Jerusalem, He took the twelve disciples aside and said, \v 18 \wj “Look, we are going up to Jerusalem, and the Son of Man will be delivered over to the chief priests and scribes. They will condemn Him to death\wj* \v 19 \wj and will deliver Him \add over\add* to the Gentiles to be mocked and flogged and crucified. And on the third day He will be raised \add to life\add*.”\wj*
\s1 A Mother’s Request
\r (\ref Mark 10:35–45|MRK 10:35-45\ref*)
\p \v 20 Then the mother of Zebedee’s sons came to \add Jesus\add* with her sons \add and\add* knelt down to make a request of Him.
\p \v 21 \wj “What do you want?”\wj* He inquired.
\p She answered, “Declare that in Your kingdom one of these two sons of mine may sit at Your right hand, and \add the other\add* at Your left.”
\p \v 22 \wj “You do not know what you are asking,”\wj* Jesus replied. \wj “Can you drink the cup I am going to drink?”\wj*\f + \fr 20:22 \ft BYZ includes \fqa or be baptized with the baptism with which I am baptized. \ft TR is similar.\f*
\p “We can,” \add the brothers\add* answered.
\p \v 23 \wj “You will indeed drink My cup,”\wj*\f + \fr 20:23 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa and be baptized with the baptism with which I am baptized\ft .\f* \add Jesus\add* said. \wj “But to sit at My right \add or\add* left is not Mine to grant. \add These seats belong\add* to those for whom My Father has prepared them.”\wj*
\p \v 24 When the ten heard about \add this\add*, they were indignant with the two brothers. \v 25 But Jesus called them aside \add and\add* said, \wj “You know that the rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them, and \add their\add* superiors exercise authority over them.\wj* \v 26 \wj It shall not be this way among you. Instead, whoever wants to become great among you must be your servant,\wj* \v 27 \wj and whoever wants to be first among you must be your slave —\wj* \v 28 \wj just as the Son of Man did not come to be served, but to serve, and to give His life \add as\add* a ransom for many.”\wj*
\s1 The Blind Men by the Road
\r (\ref Mark 10:46–52|MRK 10:46-52\ref*; \ref Luke 18:35–43|LUK 18:35-43\ref*)
\p \v 29 \add As\add* they were leaving Jericho, a large crowd followed Him. \v 30 And there were two blind \add men\add* sitting beside the road. When they heard that Jesus was passing by, they cried out, “Lord, Son of David, have mercy on us!”
\p \v 31 The crowd admonished them to be silent, but they cried out all the louder, “Lord, Son of David, have mercy on us!”
\p \v 32 Jesus stopped \add and\add* called them. \wj “What do you want Me to do for you?”\wj* He asked.
\p \v 33 “Lord,” they answered, “let our eyes be opened.”
\p \v 34 Moved with compassion, Jesus touched their eyes, and at once they received their sight and followed Him.
\c 21
\s1 The Triumphal Entry
\r (\ref Zechariah 9:9–13|ZEC 9:9-13\ref*; \ref Mark 11:1–11|MRK 11:1-11\ref*; \ref Luke 19:28–40|LUK 19:28-40\ref*; \ref John 12:12–19|JHN 12:12-19\ref*)
\p \v 1 As they approached Jerusalem and came to Bethphage on the Mount of Olives, Jesus sent out two disciples, \v 2 saying to them, \wj “Go into the village ahead of you, and at once you will find a donkey tied \add there\add*, with \add her\add* colt beside her. Untie \add them and\add* bring \add them\add* to Me.\wj* \v 3 \wj If anyone questions you, tell \add him\add* that the Lord needs them, and he will send them right away.”\wj*
\p \v 4 This took place to fulfill what was spoken through the prophet:
\b 
\q1 \v 5 “Say to the Daughter of Zion,
\q2 ‘See, your King comes to you,
\q1 gentle and riding on a donkey,
\q2 on a colt, \add the\add* foal of a donkey.’”\f + \fr 21:5 \ref Zechariah 9:9|ZEC 9:9\ref*\f*
\p \v 6 So the disciples went and did as Jesus had directed them. \v 7 They brought the donkey and the colt and laid their cloaks on them, and \add Jesus\add* sat on them.
\p \v 8 A massive crowd spread their cloaks on the road, while others cut branches from the trees and spread \add them\add* on the road.
\p \v 9 The crowds that went ahead of Him and those that followed were shouting:
\b 
\q1 “Hosanna to the Son of David!”\f + \fr 21:9 \fqa Hosanna \ft is a transliteration of the Hebrew \fqa Hosia-na\ft , meaning \fqa Save, we pray \ft or \fqa Save now\ft , which became a shout of praise; see \ref Psalm 118:25|PSA 118:25\ref*; also in verse 15.\f*
\b 
\q1 “Blessed is He who comes in \add the\add* name of \add the\add* Lord!”\f + \fr 21:9 \ref Psalm 118:26|PSA 118:26\ref*\f*
\b 
\q1 “Hosanna in the highest!”\f + \fr 21:9 \ft Or \fqa “Hosanna in the highest heaven!”\ft See \ref Psalm 118:25|PSA 118:25\ref* and Psalm 148:1.\f*
\p \v 10 When \add Jesus\add* had entered Jerusalem, the whole city was stirred \add and\add* asked, “Who is this?”
\p \v 11 The crowds replied, “This is Jesus, the prophet from Nazareth in Galilee.”
\s1 Jesus Cleanses the Temple
\r (\ref Mark 11:15–19|MRK 11:15-19\ref*; \ref Luke 19:45–48|LUK 19:45-48\ref*; \ref John 2:12–25|JHN 2:12-25\ref*)
\p \v 12 Then Jesus entered the temple \add courts\add*\f + \fr 21:12 \ft Literally \fqa the temple\ft ; also in verses 15 and 23; BYZ and TR \fqa the temple of God\f* and drove out all who were buying and selling \add there\add*. He overturned the tables of the money changers and the seats of those selling doves. \v 13 And He declared to them, \wj “It is written: ‘My house will be called a house of prayer.’\f + \fr 21:13 \ref Isaiah 56:7|ISA 56:7\ref*\f* But you are making it ‘a den of robbers.’\f + \fr 21:13 \ref Jeremiah 7:11|JER 7:11\ref*\f*”\wj*
\p \v 14 \add The\add* blind and \add the\add* lame came to Him at the temple, and He healed them. \v 15 But the chief priests and scribes were indignant when they saw the wonders He performed and the children shouting in the temple \add courts\add*, “Hosanna to the Son of David!”
\p \v 16 “Do You hear what these \add children\add* are saying?” they asked.
\p \wj “Yes,”\wj* Jesus answered. \wj “Have you never read:\wj*
\q1 \wj ‘From \add the\add* mouths of children and infants\wj*
\q2 \wj You have ordained praise’\f + \fr 21:16 \ref Psalm 8:2|PSA 8:2\ref* (see also LXX)\f*?”\wj*
\p \v 17 Then He left them \add and\add* went out of the city to Bethany, where He spent the night.
\s1 The Barren Fig Tree
\r (\ref Mark 11:12–14|MRK 11:12-14\ref*; \ref Mark 11:20–25|MRK 11:20-25\ref*)
\p \v 18 In the morning, as \add Jesus\add* was returning to the city, He was hungry. \v 19 Seeing a fig tree by the road, He went up to it but found nothing on it except leaves. \wj “May you never bear fruit again!”\wj* He said. And immediately the tree withered.
\p \v 20 When the disciples saw \add this\add*, they marveled \add and\add* asked, “How \add did\add* the fig tree wither so quickly?”
\p \v 21 \wj “Truly I tell you,”\wj* Jesus replied, \wj “if you have faith and \add do\add* not doubt, not only will you do what was done to the fig tree, but even if you say to this mountain, ‘Be lifted up and thrown into the sea,’ it will happen.\wj* \v 22 \wj If you believe, you will receive whatever you ask for in prayer.”\wj*
\s1 Jesus’ Authority Challenged
\r (\ref Mark 11:27–33|MRK 11:27-33\ref*; \ref Luke 20:1–8|LUK 20:1-8\ref*)
\p \v 23 When \add Jesus\add* returned to the temple \add courts and\add* began to teach, the chief priests and elders of the people came up to Him. “By what authority are You doing these things?” they asked. “And who gave You this authority?”
\p \v 24 \wj “I will also ask you one question,”\wj* Jesus replied, \wj “\add and\add* if you answer Me, I will tell you by what authority I am doing these things.\wj* \v 25 \wj What was the source of John’s baptism? \add Was it\add* from heaven or from men?”\wj*
\p They deliberated among themselves \add and\add* said, “If we say, ‘From heaven,’ He will ask, ‘Why then did you not believe him?’ \v 26 But if we say, ‘From men,’ we are afraid of the people, for they all regard John as a prophet.” \v 27 So they answered, “We do not know.”
\p And \add Jesus\add* replied, \wj “Neither \add will\add* I tell you by what authority I am doing these things.\wj*
\s1 The Parable of the Two Sons
\p \v 28 \wj But what \add do\add* you think? \add There was\add* a man who had two sons. He went to the first \add one and\add* said, ‘Son, go \add and\add* work today in the vineyard.’\wj*
\p \v 29 \wj ‘I will not,’ he replied. But later he changed his mind \add and\add* went.\f + \fr 21:29 \ft NE and WH \fqa But he went.\f*\wj*
\p \v 30 \wj Then \add the man\add* went to the second \add son and\add* told \add him\add* the same thing.\wj*
\p \wj ‘I \add will\add*, sir,’ he said. But he did not go.\wj*
\p \v 31 \wj Which of the two did the will of \add his\add* father?”\wj*
\p “The first,\f + \fr 21:31 \ft NE and WH \fqa The latter\f*” they answered.
\p Jesus said to them, \wj “Truly I tell you, the tax collectors and prostitutes are entering the kingdom of God before you.\wj* \v 32 \wj For John came to you in \add the\add* way of righteousness, and you did not believe him, but the tax collectors and prostitutes \add did\add*. And \add even\add* after you saw this, you did not repent \add and\add* believe him.\wj*
\s1 The Parable of the Wicked Tenants
\r (\ref Mark 12:1–12|MRK 12:1-12\ref*; \ref Luke 20:9–18|LUK 20:9-18\ref*)
\p \v 33 \wj Listen to another parable: There was a landowner who planted a vineyard. He put a wall around it, dug a winepress in it, and built a tower. Then he rented it out to \add some\add* tenants and went away on a journey.\wj*
\p \v 34 \wj When the harvest time drew near, he sent his servants to the tenants to collect his \add share of the\add* fruit.\wj* \v 35 \wj But the tenants seized his servants. They beat one, killed \add another\add*, and stoned a third.\wj*
\p \v 36 \wj Again, he sent other servants, more than the first \add group\add*. But \add the tenants\add* did the same to them.\wj*
\p \v 37 \wj Finally, he sent his son to them. ‘They will respect my son,’ he said.\wj*
\p \v 38 \wj But when the tenants saw the son, they said to one another, ‘This is the heir. Come, let us kill him and take his inheritance.’\wj* \v 39 \wj So they seized him \add and\add* threw \add him\add* out of the vineyard and killed \add him\add*.\wj*
\p \v 40 \wj Therefore, when the owner of the vineyard returns, what will he do to those tenants?”\wj*
\p \v 41 “He will bring those wretches \add to a\add* wretched \add end\add*,” they replied, “and will rent out the vineyard to other tenants who will give him \add his\add* share of the fruit at harvest time.”
\p \v 42 Jesus said to them, \wj “Have you never read in the Scriptures:\wj*
\q1 \wj ‘\add The\add* stone the builders rejected\wj*
\q2 \wj has become the cornerstone.\wj*
\q1 \wj This is from \add the\add* Lord,\wj*
\q2 \wj and it is marvelous in our eyes’\f + \fr 21:42 \ref Psalm 118:22–23|PSA 118:22-23\ref*\f*?\wj*
\p \v 43 \wj Therefore I tell you that the kingdom of God will be taken away from you and given to a people who will produce its fruit.\wj* \v 44 \wj He who falls on this stone will be broken to pieces, but he on whom it falls will be crushed.\f + \fr 21:44 \ft Tischendorf and some early manuscripts do not include verse 44; see also \ref Luke 20:18|LUK 20:18\ref*.\f*”\wj*
\p \v 45 When the chief priests and Pharisees heard His parables, they knew that \add Jesus\add* was speaking about them. \v 46 Although they wanted to arrest Him, they were afraid of the crowds, because \add the people\add* regarded Him as a prophet.
\c 22
\s1 The Parable of the Banquet
\r (\ref Luke 14:15–24|LUK 14:15-24\ref*)
\p \v 1 \add Once\add* again, Jesus spoke to them in parables: \v 2 \wj “The kingdom of heaven is like a king who prepared a wedding banquet for his son.\wj* \v 3 \wj He sent his servants to call those he had invited to the banquet, but they refused to come.\wj*
\p \v 4 \wj Again, he sent other servants \add and\add* said, ‘Tell those who have been invited that I have prepared my dinner. My oxen and fattened cattle have been killed, and everything \add is\add* ready. Come to the wedding banquet.’\wj*
\p \v 5 \wj But they paid no attention \add and\add* went away, one to his field, another to his business.\wj* \v 6 \wj The rest seized his servants, mistreated \add them\add*, and killed \add them\add*.\wj*
\p \v 7 \wj The king was enraged, and he sent his troops to destroy those murderers and burn their city.\wj* \v 8 \wj Then he said to his servants, ‘The wedding banquet is ready, but those I invited were not worthy.\wj* \v 9 \wj Go therefore to the crossroads and invite to the banquet as many as you can find.’\wj*
\p \v 10 \wj So the servants went out into the streets \add and\add* gathered everyone they could find, both evil and good, and the wedding hall was filled with guests.\wj*
\p \v 11 \wj But when the king came in to see the guests, he spotted a man \add who was\add* not dressed in wedding clothes.\wj* \v 12 \wj ‘Friend,’ he asked, ‘how did you get in here without wedding clothes?’\wj*
\p \wj But \add the man\add* was speechless.\wj*
\p \v 13 \wj Then the king told the servants, ‘Tie him hand and foot, \add and\add* throw him into the outer darkness, \add where\add* there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.’\wj*
\p \v 14 \wj For many are called, but few \add are\add* chosen.”\wj*
\s1 Paying Taxes to Caesar
\r (\ref Mark 12:13–17|MRK 12:13-17\ref*; \ref Luke 20:19–26|LUK 20:19-26\ref*)
\p \v 15 Then the Pharisees went out \add and\add* conspired to trap \add Jesus\add* in His words. \v 16 They sent their disciples to Him along with the Herodians. “Teacher,” they said, “we know that You are honest and \add that\add* You teach the way of God in accordance with the truth. You seek favor from no one, because You pay no attention to external appearance. \v 17 So tell us what You think: Is it lawful to pay taxes to Caesar or not?”
\p \v 18 But Jesus knew their evil intent \add and\add* said, \wj “You hypocrites, why are you testing Me?\wj* \v 19 \wj Show Me the coin used for the tax.”\wj*
\p And they brought Him a denarius.\f + \fr 22:19 \ft A denarius was customarily a day’s wage for a laborer; see \ref Matthew 20:2|MAT 20:2\ref*.\f*
\p \v 20 \wj “Whose image \add is\add* this,”\wj* He asked, \wj “and whose inscription?”\wj*
\p \v 21 “Caesar’s,” they answered.
\p So \add Jesus\add* told them, \wj “Give to Caesar what \add is\add* Caesar’s, and to God what \add is\add* God’s.”\wj*
\p \v 22 And when they heard \add this\add*, they were amazed. So they left Him \add and\add* went away.
\s1 The Sadducees and the Resurrection
\r (\ref Mark 12:18–27|MRK 12:18-27\ref*; \ref Luke 20:27–40|LUK 20:27-40\ref*)
\p \v 23 That \add same\add* day \add the\add* Sadducees, who say there is no resurrection, came to \add Jesus\add* and questioned Him. \v 24 “Teacher,” they said, “Moses declared that if a man dies without having children, his brother is to marry the widow and raise up offspring for \add him\add*.\f + \fr 22:24 \ref Deuteronomy 25:5|DEU 25:5\ref*\f* \v 25 Now there were seven brothers among us. The first \add one\add* married and died without having children. \add So\add* he left his wife to his brother. \v 26 The same thing happened to the second and third \add brothers\add*, down to the seventh. \v 27 And last of all, the woman died. \v 28 In the resurrection, then, whose wife will she be of the seven? For all of them were married to her.”
\p \v 29 Jesus answered, \wj “You are mistaken \add because\add* you do not know the Scriptures or the power of God.\wj* \v 30 \wj In the resurrection, \add people\add* will neither marry nor be given in marriage. Instead, they will be like \add the\add* angels\f + \fr 22:30 \ft SBL, BYZ, and TR \fqa the angels of God\f* in heaven.\wj* \v 31 \wj But concerning the resurrection of the dead, have you not read what God said to you:\wj* \v 32 \wj ‘I am the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob’\f + \fr 22:32 \ref Exodus 3:6|EXO 3:6\ref*\f*? He is not the God of \add the\add* dead, but of \add the\add* living.”\wj*
\p \v 33 When the crowds heard \add this\add*, they were astonished at His teaching.
\s1 The Greatest Commandment
\r (\ref Deuteronomy 6:1–19|DEU 6:1-19\ref*; \ref Mark 12:28–34|MRK 12:28-34\ref*)
\p \v 34 And \add when\add* the Pharisees heard that \add Jesus\add* had silenced the Sadducees, they themselves gathered together. \v 35 One of them, an expert in the law, tested Him with a question: \v 36 “Teacher, which commandment \add is the\add* greatest in the Law?”
\p \v 37 \add Jesus\add* declared, \wj “‘Love \add the\add* Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your mind.’\f + \fr 22:37 \ref Deuteronomy 6:5|DEU 6:5\ref*\f*\wj* \v 38 \wj This is the first and greatest commandment.\wj* \v 39 \wj And \add the\add* second \add is\add* like it: ‘Love your neighbor as yourself.’\f + \fr 22:39 \ref Leviticus 19:18|LEV 19:18\ref*\f*\wj* \v 40 \wj All the Law and the Prophets hang on these two commandments.”\wj*
\s1 Whose Son Is the Christ?
\r (\ref Mark 12:35–37|MRK 12:35-37\ref*; \ref Luke 20:41–44|LUK 20:41-44\ref*)
\p \v 41 While the Pharisees were assembled, Jesus questioned them: \v 42 \wj “What \add do\add* you think about the Christ? Whose son is He?”\wj*
\p “David’s,” they answered.
\p \v 43 \add Jesus\add* said to them, \wj “How then \add does\add* David in \add the\add* Spirit call Him ‘Lord’? \add For\add* he says:\wj*
\q1 \v 44 \wj ‘The Lord said to my Lord,\wj*
\q2 \wj “Sit at My right hand\wj*
\q1 \wj until I put Your enemies\wj*
\q2 \wj under Your feet.”’\f + \fr 22:44 \ref Psalm 110:1|PSA 110:1\ref*\f*\wj*
\p \v 45 \wj So if David calls Him ‘Lord,’ how can He be \add David’s\add* son?”\wj*
\p \v 46 No one was able to answer a word, \add and\add* from that day \add on no one\add* dared to question Him \add any\add* further.
\c 23
\s1 Woes to Scribes and Pharisees
\r (\ref Luke 11:37–54|LUK 11:37-54\ref*)
\p \v 1 Then Jesus spoke to the crowds and to His disciples: \v 2 \wj “The scribes and Pharisees sit in Moses’ seat.\wj* \v 3 \wj So practice and observe everything they tell you. But \add do\add* not do what they do, for they do not practice what they preach.\wj* \v 4 \wj They tie up heavy, burdensome loads\f + \fr 23:4 \ft SBL, NE, and WH \fqa They tie up heavy loads\f* and lay \add them\add* on men’s shoulders, but they themselves are not willing to lift a finger to move them.\wj*
\p \v 5 \wj All their deeds are done for men to see. They broaden their phylacteries and lengthen their tassels.\wj* \v 6 \wj They love the places of honor at banquets, the chief seats in the synagogues,\wj* \v 7 \wj the greetings in the marketplaces, and the title of ‘Rabbi’ by \add which they are addressed\add*.\f + \fr 23:7 \ft Literally \fqa and to be called ‘Rabbi’ by men\f*\wj*
\p \v 8 \wj But you are not to be called ‘Rabbi,’ for you \add have\add* one Teacher, and you are all brothers.\wj* \v 9 \wj And do not call anyone on earth your father, for you \add have\add* one Father, who \add is\add* in heaven.\wj* \v 10 \wj Nor are you to be called instructors, for you \add have\add* one Instructor, the Christ.\wj* \v 11 \wj The greatest among you shall be your servant.\wj* \v 12 \wj For whoever exalts himself will be humbled, and whoever humbles himself will be exalted.\wj*
\p \v 13 \wj Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, you hypocrites! You shut the kingdom of heaven in men’s faces. You yourselves do not enter, nor will you let in those who wish to enter.\f + \fr 23:13 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa \fv 14\fv*Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, you hypocrites! You defraud widows of their houses, and for a show make lengthy prayers. Therefore you will receive greater condemnation.\ft See \ref Mark 12:40|MRK 12:40\ref* and Luke 20:47.\f*\wj*
\p \v 15 \wj Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, you hypocrites! You traverse \add land\add* and sea to win a single convert, and when he becomes \add one\add*, you make him twice as much a son of hell\f + \fr 23:15 \ft Greek \fqa Gehenna\ft ; also in verse 33\f* as you are.\wj*
\p \v 16 \wj Woe to you, blind guides! \add You\add* say, ‘If anyone swears by the temple, it means nothing; but if anyone swears by the gold of the temple, he is bound by his oath.’\wj* \v 17 \wj \add You\add* blind fools! Which is greater: the gold, or the temple that makes it sacred?\wj* \v 18 \wj And \add you say\add*, ‘If anyone swears by the altar, it means nothing; but if anyone swears by the gift on it, he is bound by his oath.’\wj* \v 19 \wj You blind men! Which \add is\add* greater: the gift, or the altar that makes it sacred?\wj* \v 20 \wj So then, he who swears by the altar swears by it and by everything on it.\wj* \v 21 \wj And he who swears by the temple swears by it and by the \add One\add* who dwells in it.\wj* \v 22 \wj And he who swears by heaven swears by God’s throne and by the \add One\add* who sits on it.\wj*
\p \v 23 \wj Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, you hypocrites! You pay tithes of mint, dill, and cumin. But you have disregarded the weightier \add matters\add* of the law: justice, mercy, and faithfulness. You should have practiced the latter, without neglecting the former.\wj* \v 24 \wj You blind guides! You strain out a gnat but swallow a camel.\f + \fr 23:24 \ft See \ref Leviticus 11:4|LEV 11:4\ref* and Leviticus 11:23, where camels and gnats are both forbidden as food.\f*\wj*
\p \v 25 \wj Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, you hypocrites! You clean the outside of the cup and dish, but inside they are full of greed and self-indulgence.\wj* \v 26 \wj Blind Pharisee! First clean the inside of the cup and dish,\f + \fr 23:26 \ft NA does not include \fqa and dish\ft .\f* so that the outside may become clean as well.\wj*
\p \v 27 \wj Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, you hypocrites! You are like whitewashed tombs, which look beautiful on the outside but on the inside are full of dead \add men’s\add* bones and every kind of impurity.\wj* \v 28 \wj In the same way, on the outside you appear to be righteous, but on the inside you are full of hypocrisy and wickedness.\wj*
\p \v 29 \wj Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, you hypocrites! You build tombs for the prophets and decorate the monuments of the righteous.\wj* \v 30 \wj And you say, ‘If we had lived in the days of our fathers, we would not have been partners with them in \add shedding\add* the blood of the prophets.’\wj* \v 31 \wj So you testify against yourselves that you are \add the\add* sons of those who murdered the prophets.\wj* \v 32 \wj Fill up, then,\f + \fr 23:32 \ft Or \fqa Go ahead, then, and complete\f* the measure \add of the sin\add* of your fathers.\wj* \v 33 \wj You snakes! You brood of vipers! How will you escape the sentence of hell?\wj*
\p \v 34 \wj Because of this, I am sending you prophets and wise \add men\add* and teachers. Some of them you will kill and crucify, and \add others\add* you will flog in your synagogues and persecute from town to town.\wj* \v 35 \wj And so upon you will come all \add the\add* righteous blood shed on earth, from the blood of righteous Abel to the blood of Zechariah son of Berechiah, whom you murdered between the temple and the altar.\wj* \v 36 \wj Truly I tell you, all these things will come upon this generation.\wj*
\s1 Lament over Jerusalem
\r (\ref Luke 13:31–35|LUK 13:31-35\ref*)
\p \v 37 \wj O Jerusalem, Jerusalem, who kills the prophets and stones those sent to her, how often I have longed to gather your children \add together\add*, as a hen gathers her chicks under \add her\add* wings, but you were unwilling!\wj* \v 38 \wj Look, your house is left to you desolate.\f + \fr 23:38 \ft NE and WH do not include \fqa desolate\ft .\f*\wj* \v 39 \wj For I tell you \add that\add* you will not see Me again until you say, ‘Blessed \add is\add* He who comes in \add the\add* name of \add the\add* Lord.’\f + \fr 23:39 \ref Psalm 118:26|PSA 118:26\ref*\f*”\wj*
\c 24
\s1 Temple Destruction and Other Signs
\r (\ref Mark 13:1–8|MRK 13:1-8\ref*; \ref Luke 21:5–9|LUK 21:5-9\ref*)
\p \v 1 \add As\add* Jesus left the temple \add and\add* was walking away, His disciples came up to \add Him\add* to point out \add its\add* buildings.
\p \v 2 \wj “Do you see all these things?”\wj* He replied. \wj “Truly I tell you, not one stone here will be left on \add another\add*; \add every one\add* will be thrown down.”\wj*
\p \v 3 \add While Jesus\add* was sitting on the Mount of Olives, the disciples came to Him privately. “Tell us,” they said, “when \add will\add* these things happen, and what \add will be\add* the sign of Your coming and of the end of the age?”
\p \v 4 Jesus answered, \wj “See to it that no one deceives you.\wj* \v 5 \wj For many will come in My name, claiming, ‘I am the Christ,’ and will deceive many.\wj* \v 6 \wj You will hear of wars and rumors of wars, but see to it that you are not alarmed. \add These things\add* must happen, but the end is still to come.\wj* \v 7 \wj Nation will rise against nation, and kingdom against kingdom. There will be famines and earthquakes in \add various\add* places.\wj* \v 8 \wj All these \add are the\add* beginning of birth pains.\wj*
\s1 Witnessing to All Nations
\r (\ref Mark 13:9–13|MRK 13:9-13\ref*; \ref Luke 21:10–19|LUK 21:10-19\ref*)
\p \v 9 \wj Then they will deliver you over to be persecuted and killed, and you will be hated by all nations because of My name.\wj* \v 10 \wj At that time many will fall away and will betray and hate one another,\wj* \v 11 \wj and many false prophets will arise and deceive many.\wj*
\p \v 12 \wj Because of the multiplication of wickedness, the love of most will grow cold.\wj* \v 13 \wj But the \add one who\add* perseveres to \add the\add* end will be saved.\wj*
\p \v 14 \wj And this gospel of the kingdom will be preached in all the world as a testimony to all nations, and then the end will come.\wj*
\s1 The Abomination of Desolation
\r (\ref Mark 13:14–23|MRK 13:14-23\ref*; \ref Luke 21:20–24|LUK 21:20-24\ref*)
\p \v 15 \wj So when you see standing in \add the\add* holy place ‘the abomination of desolation,’\f + \fr 24:15 \ft See \ref Daniel 9:27|DAN 9:27\ref*, Daniel 11:31, and Daniel 12:11.\f* spoken of by the prophet Daniel (\add let\add* the reader understand),\wj* \v 16 \wj then \add let\add* those who are in Judea flee to the mountains.\wj* \v 17 \wj \add Let no one\add* on the housetop come down to retrieve anything from his house.\wj* \v 18 \wj And \add let no one\add* in the field return for his cloak.\wj*
\p \v 19 \wj How miserable those days \add will be\add* for pregnant and nursing \add mothers\add*!\wj* \v 20 \wj Pray that your flight will not occur in the winter \add or\add* on the Sabbath.\wj* \v 21 \wj For at that time there will be great tribulation, unseen from \add the\add* beginning of \add the\add* world until now, \add and\add* never to be \add seen again\add*.\wj* \v 22 \wj If those days had not been cut short, nobody would be saved. But for the sake of the elect, those days will be cut short.\wj*
\p \v 23 \wj At that time, if anyone says to you, ‘Look, here \add is\add* the Christ!’ or ‘There \add He is\add*!’ \add do\add* not believe \add it\add*.\wj* \v 24 \wj For false Christs and false prophets will appear and perform great signs and wonders to deceive even the elect, if that were possible.\wj* \v 25 \wj See, I have told you in advance.\wj*
\s1 The Return of the Son of Man
\r (\ref Mark 13:24–27|MRK 13:24-27\ref*; \ref Luke 21:25–28|LUK 21:25-28\ref*)
\p \v 26 \wj So if they tell you, ‘There He is, in the wilderness,’ \add do\add* not go out, \add or\add*, ‘Here \add He is,\add* in the inner rooms,’ \add do\add* not believe \add it\add*.\wj* \v 27 \wj For just as the lightning comes from \add the\add* east and flashes as far as \add the\add* west, so will be the coming of the Son of Man.\wj* \v 28 \wj Wherever there is a carcass, there the vultures will gather.\wj*
\p \v 29 \wj Immediately after the tribulation of those days:\wj*
\q1 \wj ‘The sun will be darkened,\wj*
\q2 \wj and the moon will not give its light;\wj*
\q1 \wj the stars will fall from the sky,\wj*
\q2 \wj and the powers of the heavens will be shaken.\f + \fr 24:29 \ft Or \fqa and the celestial bodies will be shaken\ft ; see \ref Isaiah 13:10|ISA 13:10\ref*, Isaiah 34:4, and Joel 2:10.\f*’\wj*
\p \v 30 \wj At that time the sign of the Son of Man will appear in heaven,\f + \fr 24:30 \ft Or \fqa the sky\ft ; twice in this verse\f* and all the tribes of the earth will mourn. They will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven with power and great glory.\f + \fr 24:30 \ft See \ref Daniel 7:13–14|DAN 7:13-14\ref*.\f*\wj* \v 31 \wj And He will send out His angels with a loud trumpet call, and they will gather His elect from the four winds, from one end of the heavens to the \add other\add*.\wj*
\s1 The Lesson of the Fig Tree
\r (\ref Mark 13:28–31|MRK 13:28-31\ref*; \ref Luke 21:29–33|LUK 21:29-33\ref*)
\p \v 32 \wj Now learn this lesson\f + \fr 24:32 \ft Or \fqa this parable\f* from the fig tree: As soon as its branches become tender and sprout leaves, you know that summer \add is\add* near.\wj* \v 33 \wj So also, when you see all these things, you will know that He is near,\f + \fr 24:33 \ft Or \fqa it is near\f* right at \add the\add* door.\wj* \v 34 \wj Truly I tell you, this generation \add will\add* not pass away until all these things have happened.\wj* \v 35 \wj Heaven and earth will pass away, but My words \add will\add* never pass away.\wj*
\s1 Readiness at Any Hour
\r (\ref Genesis 6:1–7|GEN 6:1-7\ref*; \ref Mark 13:32–37|MRK 13:32-37\ref*; \ref Luke 12:35–48|LUK 12:35-48\ref*)
\p \v 36 \wj No one knows about that day \add or\add* hour, not even the angels in heaven, nor the Son,\f + \fr 24:36 \ft BYZ and TR do not include \fqa nor the Son\ft .\f* but only the Father.\wj* \v 37 \wj As \add it was\add* in the days of Noah, so will it be at the coming of the Son of Man.\wj* \v 38 \wj For in the days before the flood, \add people\add* were eating and drinking, marrying and giving in marriage, up to the day Noah entered the ark.\wj* \v 39 \wj And they were oblivious until the flood came and swept them all away. So will it be \add at\add* the coming of the Son of Man.\wj* \v 40 \wj Two \add men\add* will be in the field: one will be taken and \add the other\add* left.\wj* \v 41 \wj Two \add women\add* will be grinding at the mill: one will be taken and \add the other\add* left.\wj*
\p \v 42 \wj Therefore keep watch, because you do not know \add the\add* day on which your Lord will come.\wj* \v 43 \wj But understand this: If the homeowner had known in which watch of the night the thief was coming, he would have kept watch and \add would\add* not have let his house be broken into.\wj* \v 44 \wj For this reason, you also must be ready, because the Son of Man will come at an hour you do not expect.\wj*
\p \v 45 \wj Who then is the faithful and wise servant, whom the master has put in charge of his household, to give \add the others their\add* food at the proper time?\wj* \v 46 \wj Blessed \add is\add* that servant whose master finds him doing so when he returns.\wj* \v 47 \wj Truly I tell you, he will put him in charge of all his possessions.\wj*
\p \v 48 \wj But suppose that servant \add is\add* wicked \add and\add* says in his heart, ‘My master will be away a long time.’\wj* \v 49 \wj And he begins to beat his fellow servants and to eat and drink with drunkards.\wj* \v 50 \wj The master of that servant will come on a day he does not expect and at an hour he does not anticipate.\wj* \v 51 \wj Then he will cut him to pieces and assign him a place with the hypocrites, \add where\add* there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.’’\wj*
\c 25
\s1 The Parable of the Ten Virgins
\p \v 1 \wj \add “\add*At that time the kingdom of heaven will be like ten virgins who took their lamps \add and\add* went out to meet the bridegroom.\wj* \v 2 \wj Five of them were foolish, and five \add were\add* wise.\wj* \v 3 \wj The foolish \add ones\add* took their lamps \add but did not\add* take along \add any extra\add* oil.\wj* \v 4 \wj But the wise \add ones\add* took oil in flasks along with their lamps.\wj* \v 5 \wj \add When\add* the bridegroom was delayed, they all became drowsy and fell asleep.\wj*
\p \v 6 \wj At midnight the cry rang out: ‘Here is the bridegroom! Come out to meet him!’\wj*
\p \v 7 \wj Then all the virgins woke up and trimmed their lamps.\wj* \v 8 \wj The foolish ones said to the wise, ‘Give us some of your oil; our lamps are going out.’\wj*
\p \v 9 \wj ‘No,’ said the wise \add ones\add*, ‘or there may not be enough for \add both\add* us and you. Instead, go to those who sell \add oil\add* and buy \add some\add* for yourselves.’\wj*
\p \v 10 \wj But \add while\add* they were on their way to buy \add it\add*, the bridegroom arrived. Those who were ready went in with him to the wedding banquet, and the door was shut.\wj*
\p \v 11 \wj Later the other virgins arrived and said, ‘Lord, lord, open \add the door\add* for us!’\wj*
\p \v 12 \wj But he replied, ‘Truly I tell you, I do not know you.’\wj*
\p \v 13 \wj Therefore keep watch, because you do not know the day \add or\add* the hour.\f + \fr 25:13 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa when the Son of Man comes\ft .\f*\wj*
\s1 The Parable of the Talents
\r (\ref Luke 19:11–27|LUK 19:11-27\ref*)
\p \v 14 \wj For \add it\add* is just like a man going on a journey, who called his servants and entrusted them with his possessions.\wj* \v 15 \wj To one he gave five talents,\f + \fr 25:15 \ft A talent was worth about twenty years’ wages for a laborer.\f* to \add another\add* two \add talents\add*, and to \add another\add* one \add talent\add* — each according to his own ability. And he went on his journey.\wj*
\p \v 16 \wj The \add servant\add* who had received the five talents went at once \add and\add* put them to work\f + \fr 25:16 \ft Or \fqa \fv 15\fv*... And he went on his journey at once. \fv 16\fv*... went and put them to work\ft . Translators vary as to the placement of the Greek adverb \fqa eutheōs \ft (at once) at the end of verse 15 or at the beginning of verse 16.\f* and gained five more.\wj* \v 17 \wj Likewise, the \add one with\add* the two \add talents\add* gained two more.\wj* \v 18 \wj But the \add servant\add* who had received the one \add talent\add* went off, dug a hole in the ground, and hid his master’s money.\wj*
\p \v 19 \wj After a long time the master of those servants returned and settled accounts with them.\wj* \v 20 \wj The \add servant\add* who had received the five talents came \add and\add* presented five more. ‘Master,’ he said, ‘you entrusted me \add with\add* five talents. See, I have gained five more.’\wj*
\p \v 21 \wj His master replied, ‘Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful with a few things; I will put you \add in charge\add* of many things. Enter into the joy of your master!’\wj*
\p \v 22 \wj The \add servant who had received\add* the two talents also came \add and\add* said, ‘Master, you entrusted me \add with\add* two talents. See, I have gained two more.’\wj*
\p \v 23 \wj His master replied, ‘Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful with a few things; I will put you \add in charge\add* of many things. Enter into the joy of your master!’\wj*
\p \v 24 \wj Finally, the \add servant\add* who had received the one talent came and said, ‘Master, I knew that you are a hard man, reaping where you have not sown and gathering where you have not scattered seed.\wj* \v 25 \wj So I was afraid and went out \add and\add* hid your talent in the ground. See, you have what \add belongs\add* to you.’\wj*
\p \v 26 \wj ‘You wicked, lazy servant!’ replied his master. ‘You knew that I reap where I have not sown and gather where I have not scattered seed.\wj* \v 27 \wj Then you should have deposited my money with the bankers, and on my return I would have received it \add back\add* with interest.\wj*
\p \v 28 \wj Therefore take the talent from him and give \add it\add* to the \add one\add* who has ten talents.\wj* \v 29 \wj For everyone who has will be given \add more\add*, and \add he\add* will have an abundance. But the \add one who does\add* not have, even what he has will be taken away from him.\wj* \v 30 \wj And throw that worthless servant into the outer darkness, \add where\add* there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.’\wj*
\s1 The Sheep and the Goats
\p \v 31 \wj When the Son of Man comes in His glory, and all the angels with Him, He will sit on His glorious throne.\wj* \v 32 \wj All the nations will be gathered before Him, and He will separate \add the people\add* one from another, as a shepherd separates the sheep from the goats.\wj* \v 33 \wj He will place the sheep on His right and the goats on \add His\add* left.\wj*
\p \v 34 \wj Then the King will say to those on His right, ‘Come, you who are blessed by My Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from \add the\add* foundation of \add the\add* world.\wj* \v 35 \wj For I was hungry and you gave Me \add something\add* to eat, I was thirsty and you gave Me \add something\add* to drink, I was a stranger and you took Me in,\wj* \v 36 \wj \add I was\add* naked and you clothed Me, I was sick and you looked after Me, I was in prison and you visited Me.’\wj*
\p \v 37 \wj Then the righteous will answer Him, ‘Lord, when did we see You hungry and feed \add You\add*, or thirsty and give \add You\add* something to drink?\wj* \v 38 \wj When did we see You a stranger and take \add You\add* in, or naked and clothe \add You\add*?\wj* \v 39 \wj When did we see You sick or in prison and visit You?’\wj*
\p \v 40 \wj And the King will reply, ‘Truly I tell you, whatever you did for one of the least of these brothers of Mine, you did for Me.’\wj*
\p \v 41 \wj Then He will say to those on \add His\add* left, ‘Depart from Me, \add you\add* who are cursed, into the eternal fire prepared for the devil and his angels.\wj* \v 42 \wj For I was hungry and you gave Me nothing to eat, I was thirsty and you gave Me nothing to drink,\wj* \v 43 \wj I was a stranger and you did not take Me in, \add I was\add* naked and you did not clothe Me, \add I was\add* sick and in prison and you did not look after Me.’\wj*
\p \v 44 \wj And they too will reply, ‘Lord, when did we see You hungry or thirsty or a stranger or naked or sick or in prison, and did not minister to You?’\wj*
\p \v 45 \wj Then \add the King\add* will answer, ‘Truly I tell you, whatever you did not do for one of the least of these, you did not do for Me.’\wj*
\p \v 46 \wj And they will go away into eternal punishment, but the righteous into eternal life.”\wj*
\c 26
\s1 The Plot to Kill Jesus
\r (\ref Mark 14:1–2|MRK 14:1-2\ref*; \ref Luke 22:1–2|LUK 22:1-2\ref*; \ref John 11:45–57|JHN 11:45-57\ref*)
\p \v 1 When Jesus had finished \add saying\add* all these things, He told His disciples, \v 2 \wj “You know that the Passover is two days away, and the Son of Man will be handed over to be crucified.”\wj*
\p \v 3 At that time the chief priests and elders of the people assembled in the courtyard of the high priest, whose name was Caiaphas, \v 4 and they conspired to arrest Jesus covertly and kill \add Him\add*. \v 5 “But not during the feast,” they said, “or there may be a riot among the people.”
\s1 Jesus Anointed at Bethany
\r (\ref Mark 14:3–9|MRK 14:3-9\ref*; \ref Luke 7:36–50|LUK 7:36-50\ref*; \ref John 12:1–8|JHN 12:1-8\ref*)
\p \v 6 While Jesus was in Bethany in \add the\add* home of Simon the Leper,\f + \fr 26:6 \ft Aramaic \fqa Simon the Potter \ft or \fqa Simon the Jar Maker\f* \v 7 a woman came to Him with an alabaster jar of expensive perfume, which she poured on His head as He reclined at the table.
\p \v 8 When the disciples saw this, they were indignant and asked, “Why this waste? \v 9 This \add perfume\add* could have been sold at a high price, and \add the money\add* given to \add the\add* poor.”
\p \v 10 Aware of this, Jesus asked, \wj “Why are you bothering \add this\add* woman? She has done a beautiful deed to Me.\wj* \v 11 \wj The poor you will always have with you,\f + \fr 26:11 \ft See \ref Deuteronomy 15:11|DEU 15:11\ref*.\f* but \add you will\add* not always have Me.\wj* \v 12 \wj By pouring this perfume on Me, she has prepared My body for burial.\wj* \v 13 \wj Truly I tell you, wherever this gospel is preached in all the world, what she has done will also be told in memory of her.”\wj*
\s1 Judas Agrees to Betray Jesus
\r (\ref Mark 14:10–11|MRK 14:10-11\ref*; \ref Luke 22:3–6|LUK 22:3-6\ref*)
\p \v 14 Then one of the Twelve, the \add one\add* called Judas Iscariot, went to the chief priests \v 15 \add and\add* asked, “What are you willing to give me if I hand Him \add over\add* to you?” And they set out for him thirty pieces of silver. \v 16 So from then on \add Judas\add* looked for an opportunity to betray \add Jesus\add*.
\s1 Preparing the Passover
\r (\ref Mark 14:12–16|MRK 14:12-16\ref*; \ref Luke 22:7–13|LUK 22:7-13\ref*)
\p \v 17 On the first \add day\add* of the Feast of Unleavened \add Bread\add*,\f + \fr 26:17 \ft Literally \fqa On the first of the Unleavened\ft ; see \ref Exodus 12:14–20|EXO 12:14-20\ref*.\f* the disciples came to Jesus \add and\add* asked, “Where do You want us to prepare for You to eat the Passover?”
\p \v 18 He answered, \wj “Go into the city to a certain man and tell him \add that\add* the Teacher says, ‘My time is near. I will keep the Passover with My disciples at your \add house\add*.’”\wj* \v 19 So the disciples did as Jesus had directed them and prepared the Passover.
\s1 The Last Supper
\r (\ref Mark 14:17–26|MRK 14:17-26\ref*; \ref Luke 22:14–23|LUK 22:14-23\ref*; \ref 1 Corinthians 11:17–34|1CO 11:17-34\ref*)
\p \v 20 When evening came, \add Jesus\add* was reclining with the twelve disciples.\f + \fr 26:20 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa the Twelve\f* \v 21 And while they were eating, He said to them, \wj “Truly I tell you, one of you will betray Me.”\wj*
\p \v 22 They were deeply grieved \add and\add* began to ask Him one after another, “Surely not I, Lord?”
\p \v 23 \add Jesus\add* answered, \wj “The \add one\add* who has dipped \add his\add* hand into the bowl with Me will betray Me.\wj* \v 24 \wj The Son of Man will go just as it is written about Him, but woe to that man by whom \add He\add* is betrayed. It would be better for him if \add he\add* had not been born.”\wj*
\p \v 25 Then Judas, who would betray Him, said, “Surely not I, Rabbi?”
\p \add Jesus\add* answered, \wj “You have said \add it\add* yourself.”\wj*
\p \v 26 While they were eating, Jesus took bread, spoke a blessing \add and\add* broke \add it\add*, and gave \add it\add* to the disciples, saying, \wj “Take \add and\add* eat; this is My body.”\wj*
\p \v 27 Then He took \add the\add* cup, gave thanks, \add and\add* gave \add it\add* to them, saying, \wj “Drink from it, all of you.\wj* \v 28 \wj This is My blood of the covenant,\f + \fr 26:28 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa the new covenant\f* which is poured out for many for \add the\add* forgiveness of sins.\wj* \v 29 \wj I tell you, I will not drink of this fruit of the vine from now on until that day when I drink it anew with you in My Father’s kingdom.”\wj*
\p \v 30 And \add when\add* they had sung a hymn, they went out to the Mount of Olives.
\s1 Jesus Predicts Peter’s Denial
\r (\ref Zechariah 13:7–9|ZEC 13:7-9\ref*; \ref Mark 14:27–31|MRK 14:27-31\ref*; \ref Luke 22:31–38|LUK 22:31-38\ref*; \ref John 13:36–38|JHN 13:36-38\ref*)
\p \v 31 Then Jesus said to them, \wj “This very night you will all fall away on account of Me. For it is written:\wj*
\q1 \wj ‘I will strike the Shepherd,\wj*
\q2 \wj and the sheep of the flock will be scattered.’\f + \fr 26:31 \ref Zechariah 13:7|ZEC 13:7\ref*\f*\wj*
\p \v 32 \wj But after I have risen, I will go ahead of you into Galilee.”\wj*
\p \v 33 Peter said to Him, “Even if all fall away on account of You, I never will.”
\p \v 34 \wj “Truly I tell you,”\wj* Jesus declared, \wj “this very night, before \add the\add* rooster crows, you will deny Me three times.”\wj*
\p \v 35 Peter replied, “Even if I have to die with You, I will never deny You.” And all the other disciples said the same thing.
\s1 Jesus Prays at Gethsemane
\r (\ref Mark 14:32–42|MRK 14:32-42\ref*; \ref Luke 22:39–46|LUK 22:39-46\ref*)
\p \v 36 Then Jesus went with \add His disciples\add* to a place called Gethsemane, and He told \add them\add*, \wj “Sit here while I go over there \add and\add* pray.”\wj*
\p \v 37 He took with Him Peter and the two sons of Zebedee \add and\add* began to be sorrowful and deeply distressed. \v 38 Then He said to them, \wj “My soul is consumed with sorrow to the point of death. Stay here and keep watch with Me.”\wj*
\p \v 39 Going a little \add farther\add*, He fell facedown \add and\add* prayed, \wj “My Father, if it is possible, \add let\add* this cup pass from Me. Yet not as I will, but as You \add will\add*.”\wj*
\p \v 40 Then \add Jesus\add* returned to the disciples and found them sleeping. \wj “Were you not able to keep watch with Me \add for\add* one hour?”\wj* He asked Peter. \v 41 \wj “Watch and pray so that you will not enter into temptation. For the spirit \add is\add* willing, but the body \add is\add* weak.”\wj*
\p \v 42 A second time He went away \add and\add* prayed, \wj “My Father, if this \add cup\add* cannot pass unless I drink it, \add may\add* Your will be done.”\wj* \v 43 And again \add Jesus\add* returned \add and\add* found them sleeping, for their eyes were heavy.
\p \v 44 So He left them \add and\add* went away once more \add and\add* prayed \add a\add* third time, saying the same thing. \v 45 Then He returned to the disciples and said, \wj “Are you still sleeping and resting? Look, the hour is near, and the Son of Man is betrayed into \add the\add* hands of sinners.\wj* \v 46 \wj Rise, let us go! See, My betrayer is approaching!”\wj*
\s1 The Betrayal of Jesus
\r (\ref Mark 14:43–52|MRK 14:43-52\ref*; \ref Luke 22:47–53|LUK 22:47-53\ref*; \ref John 18:1–14|JHN 18:1-14\ref*)
\p \v 47 While \add Jesus\add* was still speaking, Judas, one of the Twelve, arrived, accompanied by a large crowd \add armed\add* with swords and clubs, \add sent\add* from the chief priests and elders of the people.
\p \v 48 Now the betrayer had arranged a signal with them: “The One I kiss is \add the man\add*; arrest Him.” \v 49 Going directly to Jesus, he said, “Greetings, Rabbi!” and kissed Him.
\p \v 50 \wj “Friend,”\wj* Jesus replied, \wj “\add do\add* what you came for.”\wj*\f + \fr 26:50 \ft Or \fqa “Friend,” Jesus replied, “for what have you come?”\f*
\p Then \add the men\add* stepped forward, seized Jesus, and arrested Him. \v 51 At this, one of Jesus’ companions drew his sword and struck the servant of the high priest, cutting off his ear.
\p \v 52 \wj “Put your sword back in its place,”\wj* Jesus said to him. \wj “For all who draw \add the\add* sword will die by \add the\add* sword.\wj* \v 53 \wj Are you not aware that I can call on My Father, and He will at once put at My disposal more than twelve legions of angels?\wj* \v 54 \wj But how then \add would\add* the Scriptures be fulfilled that say it must happen this way?”\wj*
\p \v 55 At that time Jesus said to the crowd, \wj “Have you come out with swords and clubs to arrest Me as \add you would\add* an outlaw? Every day I sat teaching in the temple \add courts\add*,\f + \fr 26:55 \ft Literally \fqa the temple\f* and you did not arrest Me.\wj* \v 56 \wj But this \add has\add* all happened so that the writings of the prophets would be fulfilled.”\wj*
\p Then all the disciples deserted Him \add and\add* fled.
\s1 Jesus before the Sanhedrin
\r (\ref Mark 14:53–65|MRK 14:53-65\ref*; \ref Luke 22:66–71|LUK 22:66-71\ref*; \ref John 18:19–24|JHN 18:19-24\ref*)
\p \v 57 Those who had arrested Jesus led \add Him\add* away to \add the house of\add* Caiaphas the high priest, where the scribes and elders had gathered. \v 58 But Peter followed Him at a distance, right up to the courtyard of the high priest. And he went in \add and\add* sat down with the guards to see the outcome.
\p \v 59 Now the chief priests and the whole Sanhedrin\f + \fr 26:59 \ft Or \fqa the whole Council\f* were seeking false testimony against Jesus in order to put Him to death. \v 60 But they did not find any, \add though\add* many false witnesses came forward.
\p Finally two came forward \v 61 \add and\add* declared, “This \add man\add* said, ‘I am able to destroy the temple of God and rebuild it in three days.’”
\p \v 62 So the high priest stood up \add and\add* asked Him, “Have You no answer? What \add are\add* these \add men\add* testifying against You?”
\p \v 63 But Jesus remained silent.
\p Then the high priest said to Him, “I charge You under oath by the living God: Tell us if You are the Christ, the Son of God.”
\p \v 64 \wj “You have said \add it\add* yourself,”\wj* Jesus answered. \wj “But I say to all of you, from now on you will see the Son of Man sitting at \add the\add* right hand of Power\f + \fr 26:64 \ft Or \fqa the right hand of the Mighty One\f* and coming on the clouds of heaven.”\wj*\f + \fr 26:64 \ft See \ref Psalm 110:1|PSA 110:1\ref* and Daniel 7:13.\f*
\p \v 65 At this, the high priest tore his clothes \add and\add* declared, “He has blasphemed! Why do we need any more witnesses? Look, now you have heard the blasphemy. \v 66 What \add do\add* you think?”
\p “He deserves to die,” they answered.
\p \v 67 Then they spit in His face and struck Him. Others slapped \add Him\add* \v 68 \add and\add* said, “Prophesy to us, Christ! Who hit You?”
\s1 Peter Denies Jesus
\r (\ref Mark 14:66–72|MRK 14:66-72\ref*; \ref Luke 22:54–62|LUK 22:54-62\ref*; \ref John 18:15–18|JHN 18:15-18\ref*)
\p \v 69 Meanwhile, Peter was sitting out in the courtyard, and a servant girl came up to him. “You also were with Jesus the Galilean,” she said.
\p \v 70 But he denied \add it\add* before \add them\add* all: “I do not know what you are talking about.”
\p \v 71 When \add Peter\add* had gone out to the gateway, another \add servant girl\add* saw him and said to the people there, “This \add man\add* was with Jesus of Nazareth.”
\p \v 72 And again he denied \add it\add* with an oath: “I do not know the man!”
\p \v 73 After a little while, those standing nearby came up to Peter. “Surely you are one of them,” they said, “for your accent gives you away.”
\p \v 74 At that he began to curse and swear \add to them\add*, “I do not know the man!”
\p And immediately a rooster crowed.
\p \v 75 Then Peter remembered the word \add that\add* Jesus had spoken: \wj “Before \add the\add* rooster crows, you will deny Me three times.”\wj* And he went outside \add and\add* wept bitterly.
\c 27
\s1 Jesus Delivered to Pilate
\r (\ref Mark 15:1–5|MRK 15:1-5\ref*)
\p \v 1 When morning came, all the chief priests and elders of the people conspired against Jesus to put Him to death. \v 2 They bound Him, led \add Him\add* away, and handed \add Him\add* over to Pilate the governor.
\s1 Judas Hangs Himself
\r (\ref Zechariah 11:10–17|ZEC 11:10-17\ref*)
\p \v 3 When Judas, who had betrayed Him, saw that \add Jesus\add* was condemned, he was filled with remorse \add and\add* returned the thirty pieces of silver to the chief priests and elders. \v 4 “I have sinned by betraying innocent blood,” he said.
\p “What \add is that\add* to us?” they replied. “You bear the responsibility.”
\p \v 5 So \add Judas\add* threw the silver into the temple and left. Then he went away and hanged himself.
\p \v 6 The chief priests picked up the pieces of silver \add and\add* said, “It is unlawful to put this into the treasury, since it is blood money.” \v 7 \add After\add* conferring together, \add they used the money\add* to buy the potter’s field as a burial place for foreigners. \v 8 That is why \add it\add* has been called the Field of Blood to this day. \v 9 Then what was spoken through Jeremiah the prophet was fulfilled:
\b 
\q1 “They took the thirty pieces of silver,
\q2 the price set on Him by \add the\add* people of Israel,
\q1 \v 10 and they gave them for the potter’s field,
\q2 as \add the\add* Lord had commanded me.”\f + \fr 27:10 \ft See \ref Jeremiah 19:1–15|JER 19:1-15\ref*, Jeremiah 32:6–9, and Zechariah 11:12–13.\f*
\s1 Jesus before Pilate
\r (\ref Luke 23:1–5|LUK 23:1-5\ref*; \ref John 18:28–40|JHN 18:28-40\ref*)
\p \v 11 Meanwhile Jesus stood before the governor, who questioned Him: “Are You the King of the Jews?”
\p \wj “You have said \add so\add*,”\wj* Jesus replied.
\p \v 12 And when He was accused by the chief priests and elders, He gave no answer.
\p \v 13 Then Pilate asked Him, “Do You not hear how many charges they are bringing against You?”
\p \v 14 But \add Jesus\add* gave no answer, not even to a single charge, much to the governor’s amazement.
\s1 The Crowd Chooses Barabbas
\r (\ref Mark 15:6–11|MRK 15:6-11\ref*; \ref Luke 23:13–25|LUK 23:13-25\ref*)
\p \v 15 Now \add it was\add* the governor’s custom at \add the\add* feast to release to the crowd a prisoner of their choosing. \v 16 At that time they were holding a notorious prisoner named Barabbas.\f + \fr 27:16 \ft SBL and NA \fqa Jesus Barabbas\ft ; also in verse 17, but universally called \fqa Barabbas \ft in verses 20, 21, and 26\f* \v 17 So when \add the crowd\add* had assembled, Pilate asked them, “Which \add one\add* do you want me to release to you: Barabbas, or Jesus who is called Christ?” \v 18 For he knew \add it was\add* out of envy \add that\add* they had handed \add Jesus\add* over to him.
\p \v 19 \add While Pilate\add* was sitting on the judgment seat, his wife sent him this message: “Have nothing to do with that innocent \add man\add*, for I have suffered terribly in a dream today because of Him.”
\p \v 20 But the chief priests and elders persuaded the crowds to ask for Barabbas and to have Jesus put to death.
\p \v 21 “Which of the two do you want me to release to you?” asked the governor.
\p “Barabbas,” they replied.
\p \v 22 “What then should I do with Jesus who is called Christ?” Pilate asked.
\p They all answered, “Crucify Him!”
\p \v 23 “Why?” asked \add Pilate\add*. “What evil has He done?”
\p But they shouted all the louder, “Crucify Him!”
\s1 Pilate Washes His Hands
\r (\ref Mark 15:12–15|MRK 15:12-15\ref*)
\p \v 24 When Pilate saw that he was accomplishing nothing, but that instead a riot was breaking out, he took water \add and\add* washed \add his\add* hands before the crowd. “I am innocent of this \add man’s\add* blood,\f + \fr 27:24 \ft Literally \fqa this blood\ft ; BYZ and TR \fqa this righteous blood\f*” he said. “You bear the responsibility.”
\p \v 25 All the people answered, “His blood \add be\add* on us and on our children!”
\p \v 26 \add So Pilate\add* released Barabbas to them. But he had Jesus flogged, \add and\add* handed Him over to be crucified.
\s1 The Soldiers Mock Jesus
\r (\ref Isaiah 50:4–11|ISA 50:4-11\ref*; \ref Mark 15:16–20|MRK 15:16-20\ref*; \ref Luke 22:63–65|LUK 22:63-65\ref*; \ref John 19:1–15|JHN 19:1-15\ref*)
\p \v 27 Then the governor’s soldiers took Jesus into the Praetorium \add and\add* gathered the whole company around Him. \v 28 They stripped Him \add and\add* put a scarlet robe on Him. \v 29 And they twisted together a crown of thorns \add and\add* set \add it\add* on His head. \add They put\add* a staff in His right hand, knelt down before Him, \add and\add* mocked Him, saying, “Hail, King of the Jews!” \v 30 Then they spit on Him \add and\add* took the staff and struck Him on the head \add repeatedly\add*.
\p \v 31 After they had mocked Him, they removed the robe and put His \add own\add* clothes \add back\add* on Him. Then they led Him away to crucify \add Him\add*.
\s1 The Crucifixion
\r (\ref Psalm 22:1–31|PSA 22:1-31\ref*; \ref Mark 15:21–32|MRK 15:21-32\ref*; \ref Luke 23:26–43|LUK 23:26-43\ref*; \ref John 19:16–27|JHN 19:16-27\ref*)
\p \v 32 Along the way they found a man from Cyrene, named Simon, \add and\add* they forced him to carry the cross of \add Jesus\add*.
\p \v 33 And \add when\add* they came to a place called Golgotha, which means The Place of the Skull, \v 34 they offered Him wine to drink, mixed with gall; but after tasting \add it\add*, He refused to drink \add it\add*.
\p \v 35 When they had crucified Him, they divided up His garments by casting lots.\f + \fr 27:35 \ft See \ref Psalm 22:18|PSA 22:18\ref*; TR includes \fqa to fulfill what was said through the prophet: “They divided My garments among them, and cast lots for My clothing.”\f* \v 36 And sitting down, they kept watch over Him there.
\p \v 37 Above His head they posted the written charge against Him:
\pc THIS IS JESUS, THE KING OF THE JEWS.
\p \v 38 Two robbers\f + \fr 27:38 \ft Or \fqa insurrectionists\ft ; also in verse 44\f* were crucified with Him, one on \add His\add* right and \add the other\add* on \add His\add* left.
\p \v 39 And those who passed by heaped abuse on Him, shaking their heads \v 40 and saying, “You who are going to destroy the temple and rebuild \add it\add* in three days, save Yourself! If You are \add the\add* Son of God, come down from the cross!”
\p \v 41 In the same way, the chief priests, scribes, and elders mocked Him, saying, \v 42 “He saved others, \add but\add* He cannot save Himself. He is \add the\add* King of Israel! Let Him come down now from the cross, and we will believe in Him. \v 43 He trusts in God. Let \add God\add* deliver Him now if He wants Him.\f + \fr 27:43 \ref Psalm 22:8|PSA 22:8\ref*\f* For He said, \wj ‘I am \add the\add* Son of God.’\wj*”
\p \v 44 In the same way, even the robbers who were crucified with Him berated Him.
\s1 The Death of Jesus
\r (\ref Psalm 22:1–31|PSA 22:1-31\ref*; \ref Mark 15:33–41|MRK 15:33-41\ref*; \ref Luke 23:44–49|LUK 23:44-49\ref*; \ref John 19:28–30|JHN 19:28-30\ref*)
\p \v 45 From \add the\add* sixth hour until \add the\add* ninth hour\f + \fr 27:45 \ft That is, from noon until three in the afternoon\f* darkness came over all the land. \v 46 About the ninth hour Jesus cried out in a loud voice, \wj “Eli, Eli,\f + \fr 27:46 \ft NE and WH \fqa Eloi, Eloi\f* lema sabachthani?”\wj* which means, \wj “My God, My God, why have You forsaken Me?”\wj*\f + \fr 27:46 \ref Psalm 22:1|PSA 22:1\ref*\f*
\p \v 47 When some of those standing there heard this, they said, “\add He\add* is calling Elijah.” \v 48 One of them quickly ran and brought a sponge. He filled \add it\add* with sour wine,\f + \fr 27:48 \ft Or \fqa filled it with wine vinegar\f* put \add it\add* on a reed, and held it up for \add Jesus\add* to drink.\f + \fr 27:48 \ft See \ref Psalm 69:21|PSA 69:21\ref*.\f*
\p \v 49 But the others said, “Leave \add Him\add* alone. Let us see if Elijah comes to save Him.”\f + \fr 27:49 \ft WH includes \fqa And another took a spear and pierced His side, and water and blood flowed out\ft ; see \ref John 19:34|JHN 19:34\ref*.\f*
\p \v 50 When Jesus had cried out again in a loud voice, He yielded up \add His\add* spirit. \v 51 At that moment the veil of the temple was torn in two from top to bottom. The earth quaked, and the rocks were split. \v 52 The tombs broke open, and \add the\add* bodies of many saints who had fallen asleep were raised. \v 53 After \add Jesus’\add* resurrection, when they had come out of the tombs, they entered the holy city and appeared to many \add people\add*.
\p \v 54 When the centurion and those with him who were guarding Jesus saw the earthquake and all that had happened, they were terrified \add and\add* said, “Truly this was \add the\add* Son of God.”
\p \v 55 And many women were there, watching from a distance. \add They\add* had followed Jesus from Galilee to minister to Him. \v 56 Among \add them\add* were Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James and Joseph, and the mother of Zebedee’s sons.
\s1 The Burial of Jesus
\r (\ref Isaiah 53:9–12|ISA 53:9-12\ref*; \ref Mark 15:42–47|MRK 15:42-47\ref*; \ref Luke 23:50–56|LUK 23:50-56\ref*; \ref John 19:38–42|JHN 19:38-42\ref*)
\p \v 57 When it was evening, there came a rich man from Arimathea named Joseph, who himself was a disciple of Jesus. \v 58 He went to Pilate to ask for the body of Jesus, and Pilate ordered that it be given to him. \v 59 So Joseph took the body, wrapped it in a clean linen cloth, \v 60 and placed it in his own new tomb that he had cut into the rock. Then he rolled a great stone across the entrance to the tomb \add and\add* went away. \v 61 Mary Magdalene and the other Mary were sitting there opposite the tomb.
\s1 The Guards at the Tomb
\p \v 62 The next day, the one after Preparation Day, the chief priests and Pharisees assembled before Pilate. \v 63 “Sir,” they said, “we remember that while He was alive that deceiver said, \wj ‘After three days I will rise again.’\wj* \v 64 So give the order that the tomb be secured until the third day. Otherwise, His disciples may come \add and\add* steal Him away and tell the people He has risen from the dead. And \add this\add* last deception would be worse than the first.”
\p \v 65 “You have a guard,” Pilate said. “Go, make \add the tomb as\add* secure as you know \add how\add*.” \v 66 So they went \add and\add* secured the tomb by sealing the stone \add and posting\add* the guard.
\c 28
\s1 The Resurrection
\r (\ref Mark 16:1–8|MRK 16:1-8\ref*; \ref Luke 24:1–12|LUK 24:1-12\ref*; \ref John 20:1–9|JHN 20:1-9\ref*)
\p \v 1 After \add the\add* Sabbath, at dawn on \add the\add* first \add day\add* of \add the\add* week,\f + \fr 28:1 \ft Literally \fqa Now after the Sabbaths, it being dawn toward the first of the Sabbaths,\f* Mary Magdalene and the other Mary went to see the tomb.
\p \v 2 Suddenly there was a great earthquake, for an angel of \add the\add* Lord descended from heaven, rolled away the stone, and sat on it. \v 3 His appearance was like lightning, and his clothes \add were\add* white as snow. \v 4 The guards trembled in fear of him and became like dead \add men\add*.
\p \v 5 But the angel said to the women, “\add Do\add* not be afraid, for I know that you are looking for Jesus, who was crucified. \v 6 He is not here; He has risen, just as He said! Come, see the place where He lay.\f + \fr 28:6 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa where the Lord lay\f* \v 7 Then go quickly \add and\add* tell His disciples, ‘He has risen from the dead and is going ahead of you into Galilee. There you will see Him.’ See, I have told you.”
\p \v 8 So they hurried away from the tomb in fear and great joy, \add and\add* ran to tell His disciples. \v 9 Suddenly\f + \fr 28:9 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa They were going to tell His disciples, and suddenly\f* Jesus met them \add and\add* said, \wj “Greetings!”\wj* They came to \add Him\add*, grasped His feet, and worshiped Him. \v 10 \wj “\add Do\add* not be afraid,”\wj* said Jesus. \wj “Go \add and\add* tell My brothers to go to Galilee. There they will see Me.”\wj*
\s1 The Report of the Guards
\p \v 11 While \add the women\add* were on their way, some of the guards went into the city \add and\add* reported to the chief priests all that had happened. \v 12 And after \add the chief priests\add* had met with the elders and formed a plan, they gave the soldiers a large sum of money \v 13 \add and\add* instructed them: “You are to say, ‘His disciples came by night \add and\add* stole Him \add away while\add* we were asleep.’ \v 14 If this \add report\add* reaches the governor, we will satisfy him and keep you out of trouble.”
\p \v 15 So the \add guards\add* took the money \add and\add* did as they were instructed. And this account has been circulated among \add the\add* Jews to this very day.
\s1 The Great Commission
\r (\ref Mark 16:14–18|MRK 16:14-18\ref*)
\p \v 16 Meanwhile, the eleven disciples went to Galilee, to the mountain Jesus had designated. \v 17 When they saw Him, they worshiped Him, but some doubted.
\p \v 18 Then Jesus came to them \add and\add* said, \wj “All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to Me.\wj* \v 19 \wj Therefore go \add and\add* make disciples\f + \fr 28:19 \ft Literally \fqa Having gone, therefore, make disciples\f* of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit,\wj* \v 20 \wj \add and\add* teaching them to obey all that I have commanded you. And surely I am with you always, even to the end of the age.”\wj*
